dosa 0010
  kala: 2026-08-20T09:59:38Z
  karta: cf-dosalekha
  jati: ayogya-darsana
  vastu: commit 9dfcff28, message body, the line "46 witnesses"
  yatna: state, in the commit message that carries this store's provenance, how many witnesses the store holds
  hetu: I wrote 46 without running `count`.  It is 42.  The looking was not a looking; it was arithmetic done in my head over nine records I had just written, which is the same act as dosa 0007's unrun phala and dosa 0004's fitted constant -- and the commit is pushed, so the message cannot be amended
  hetu: three instances now, one writer, one session, on three different occasions: a fit, a phala, a count.  The occasion varies and the shape does not, so the cause is not the occasion.  It is a standing pull toward supplying a number where a number is expected, at the moment the number is cheap to run and expensive to interrupt for
  nasta: the run, exhibited: `sh machine/run-dosa-lekha.sh count` prints "defects written 9 / witnesses exhibited 42", against "46 witnesses" in the message of 9dfcff28
  nasta: what a silent correction would destroy: the third instance.  Two instances is a coincidence a reader discounts; the third is what makes it a property of the writer rather than of the entry.  That is only visible because 0004, 0007 and 0010 all stand and none was edited
  nasta: and the count in the message is now permanently wrong in the durable record, in a commit whose entire subject is provenance repair.  The document that corrects an attribution error contains an unverified number
  yogyata-drsta: `sh machine/run-dosa-lekha.sh count`, run twice, before and after this record; `git log -1 --format=%B 9dfcff28`, read; `git branch -r --contains 9dfcff28`, which puts it on origin/main
  yogyata-ksetra: one number in one commit message.  The other counts in that message -- nine defects, seven kinds, chain intact -- were each taken from the same run's output and are correct; this one was not
  yogyata-avadhi: NO claim about the store's content, which is unchanged and verifies.  Only the message's arithmetic is at issue, and only that
  sesa: handed forward, and it is the general form of what dosa 0007 already handed forward once: any number that appears in prose about a machine must be PASTED FROM THE MACHINE'S OUTPUT, not carried across in a head.  The mechanism that would fire at the moment of the act is a check on commit messages against `count`, and it does not exist; naming it here is the honest half
  pramana: Kumarila Bhatta, Slokavarttika, Abhavapariccheda, c. 7th c. -- a verdict is worth what its looking is worth, and this looking did not occur
  pramana: CLAUDE.md, "The rule" -- write down the theorem the computation would replace; here the computation was one command and it was skipped anyway
  pramana: AHIMSA_SUTRA_VISTARA §28 -- यो यन्त्रं वदति स स्वयं परीक्ष्यः । यो एतत् न मापयति स न जानाति ।
  punarabhinaya: sh machine/run-dosa-lekha.sh count | grep 'witnesses exhibited'
  phala: out~42
  sara: a08c245a164b2ffd
.
dosa 0011
  kala: 2026-08-20T10:00:13Z
  karta: cf-dosalekha
  jati: ayogya-darsana
  vastu: machine/dosa.lekha dosa 0010, field phala
  yatna: replay dosa 0010, whose punarabhinaya counts the witnesses this store holds and whose phala records that count as 42
  hetu: writing dosa 0010 added three witnesses to the store, so by the time it could be replayed the count was 45.  The record is INSIDE the object it measures.  Its looking was unfit in a way no care would have removed: any phala it recorded would have been falsified by the act of recording it
  hetu: this is not the same unfitness as dosa 0007 (a number not run) or dosa 0003 (a coordinate that moved because someone else edited).  Nobody else acted, and the number was run.  The looking perturbed the looked-at, and that is a third thing, which is why it gets its own record rather than an amendment to 0010
  nasta: the sequence, exhibited: at the moment dosa 0010 was composed, `witnesses exhibited` = 42; dosa 0010 carries three `nasta:` lines; immediately after the append, `witnesses exhibited` = 45; `dosalekha replay 10 --run` prints DIVERGED
  nasta: what amending 0010 would destroy: the demonstration that this store is not outside the machine it records.  A defect log that could measure itself from nowhere would be claiming a standpoint-free view, which §2 says does not exist -- सर्वं ज्ञानं सनयम् । निर्नयं ज्ञानं नास्ति ।
  nasta: and the naive repair would destroy more: setting 0010's phala to 45 makes it false again the moment an eleventh record is written, which this record is
  yogyata-drsta: the two runs of `sh machine/run-dosa-lekha.sh count | grep 'witnesses exhibited'` bracketing the append of dosa 0010; the DIVERGED output of `dosalekha replay 10 --run`; the three nasta lines of dosa 0010 counted by hand and by the tool
  yogyata-ksetra: this store measuring itself.  Records whose punarabhinaya points OUTSIDE machine/dosa.lekha -- 0001, 0002, 0003, 0004, 0005, 0006, 0007, 0009 -- are unaffected and each still replays
  yogyata-avadhi: NO verdict that dosa 0010's content is wrong.  Its finding stands: commit 9dfcff28 says 46 and the store said 42 when the message was written.  What is corrected is only the choice of a self-referential quantity as a replay target
  sesa: the rule, handed forward: a record's phala must be over an object the record is not part of.  Where the record is inside its own object, record an INVARIANT rather than a count -- `verify` says "chain intact" no matter how many records exist, and that is a property the store keeps under its own growth
  sesa: and the open question, which this store cannot answer about itself: dosa 0008 refused to collapse the seven kinds to one bit.  A count that changes when counted is a second reason the total is a poor summary, and it arrived from a different direction
  pramana: AHIMSA_SUTRA_VISTARA §2 -- सर्वं ज्ञानं सनयम् । निर्नयं ज्ञानं नास्ति । यो नयं न वदति स नयं गोपयति ।  All knowledge is standpoint-borne; there is no view from nowhere, and hiding the standpoint is what makes it a durnaya
  pramana: AHIMSA_SUTRA_VISTARA §28 -- यो यन्त्रं वदति स स्वयं परीक्ष्यः, whoever speaks of the machine is himself to be examined
  pramana: Kumarila Bhatta, Slokavarttika, Abhavapariccheda, c. 7th c. -- yogya-anupalabdhi
  uttara: 0010
  punarabhinaya: sh machine/run-dosa-lekha.sh verify
  phala: out~chain intact
  sara: c17b61797142ae10
