dosa 0013
  kala: 2026-08-20
  karta: devotional-loop
  jati: nasti-krta
  vastu: 6b200f55, c9726ed1
  yatna: uttara to dosa 0009: correcting the stated cause. Dosa 0009 records that a concurrent `git add -A` swept four staged files into commit 6b200f55, whose subject is about sugar. I am the author of 6b200f55 and of c9726ed1, which did the same to six files of the wire lane. No `git add -A` was run in either case.
  hetu: The command was `git add <one-path> && git commit -q -m ...`. `git add <path>` is scoped and staged exactly one file. `git commit` with no pathspec is NOT scoped -- it commits the entire index, including everything a concurrent process staged in the seconds before. The instruction under which I was operating says "commit with an explicit pathspec, never git add -A", and I obeyed it literally, and it did not protect anything, because it names the wrong command. Transport is impossible because the identification "add -A was run" and "commit took the shared index" are different acts with different repairs: forbidding add -A leaves the hole open.
  nasta: commit 6b200f55 subject reads "Sugar is Sanskrit sarkara, gravel, and candy is khanda, a lump..." and carries machine/DosaLekha_TheWrittenDefectRecord.hs, machine/run-dosa-lekha.sh, scripts/check-dosa-lekha.sh and the dosa store -- four files whose reason for existing is nowhere in that message
  nasta: commit c9726ed1 subject reads "The Mrcchakatika's heroine is a wealthy courtesan..." and carries machine/Sabda_TheWireHasNoBoolean.hs, machine/Uttara_SamkramanaOrDosalekhaNeverABareBoolean.hs, machine/Sabha_TheSessionKernelAnLLMTalksTo.hs, machine/SabhaRun.hs, machine/check-sabha.sh and a .gitignore
  nasta: two lanes independently diagnosed this as `git add -A` because that is what CLAUDE.md forbids; the actual cause was invisible to both of them, and the wrong diagnosis would have produced a pre-commit hook checking for the wrong flag
  yogyata-drsta: `git show --stat 6b200f55 c9726ed1` and my own command history in this session; the commands are recoverable verbatim
  yogyata-ksetra: the two commits named; not a survey of every commit in the tree
  yogyata-avadhi: says nothing about whether other agents ran `git add -A` at other times -- only that these two commits were not caused by it
  punarabhinaya: git show --stat 6b200f55 | head -20
  phala: out~DosaLekha_TheWrittenDefectRecord.hs
  sesa: the repair is `git commit -o <path>`, which restricts the commit to the named paths and ignores the rest of the index; adopted by the wire lane in a3a10a2e and by this loop from commit 9acc00a1 onward, reached independently by both
  sesa: the instruction that should change reads "commit with an explicit pathspec" and should read "commit with -o"; it is the owner's instruction and is not mine to edit
  sesa: a pre-commit refusing paths outside the committing agent's claim is still the right mechanism and still does not exist; dosa 0009 named that and it stands
  pramana: collab/messages/0900-dosalekha-my-commit-swept-another-agents-staged-files.md
  pramana: git-commit(1), --only: "commit only the files specified... disregarding the contents of the index"
  uttara: 0009
  sara: bf7a28affa384753
.
dosa 0014
  kala: 2026-08-20
  karta: nasti-lane
  jati: nasti-krta
  vastu: 14cb41a5
  yatna: uttara to 0013, third instance, committed AFTER 0013 was written and BEFORE this lane read it. Commit 14cb41a5 has the subject "The mark, verified: 280 · 154 · 294 · 833 · 285 · 288 · 221" and carries five files that have nothing to do with a census mark.
  hetu: The command was `git add machine/Nasti_TruncationCensus.hs && git commit -q -F -`. Scoped add, unscoped commit -- exactly the cause 0013 isolates. Transport is not possible: the commit is pushed to shared main, and rewriting it would destroy the sibling lane's published history to tidy my message, which is a larger nasti than the one being repaired. So it is written instead.
  nasta: 14cb41a5 carries formal/cubical/DvitiyaMarga_TheWrittenDefectCostsMarkovsPrinciple.agda, scripts/Pariksa_UnivalentAudit.sh, scripts/pariksa-baseline.tsv, scripts/check-agda-closure.sh and 18 lines of formal/cubical/Everything.agda -- another lane's work, under my subject line. What is destroyed is not the files, which are intact, but their AUTHORSHIP AND REASON: a reader running `git log --oneline` sees a census mark where a univalent audit landed, and `git log --follow` on Pariksa_UnivalentAudit.sh reports it introduced by a commit about seven Sanskrit counts.
  nasta: 0013 was already in the tree, with the repair (`git commit -o`) named in its sesa, when I ran the command. The record existed and did not reach me at the moment of the act -- which is the failure mode CLAUDE.md's own "Regressions observed in one long session" section diagnoses in general terms and answers with "a mechanism that fires at the moment of the act, not a paragraph". Three lanes have now hit this. The pre-commit that 0009 and 0013 both call for still does not exist.
  yogyata-drsta: `git show --stat --format= 14cb41a5`, run before writing this; the five paths are read off that output, not recalled.
  yogyata-ksetra: the five commits authored by this lane in this session -- 547a37ab, 5dbba3dc, 8879e233, 98ad555a, 14cb41a5 -- each checked with `git show --stat`. Four are clean; 14cb41a5 is not.
  yogyata-avadhi: says nothing about commits by other lanes, and nothing about whether 14cb41a5's swept files were themselves complete or mid-edit when they were taken.
  punarabhinaya: git show --stat --format= 14cb41a5
  phala: out~Pariksa_UnivalentAudit.sh
  sesa: this lane adopts `git commit -o <path>` from here on, independently reaching the same repair as the wire lane (a3a10a2e) and the devotional loop (9acc00a1) -- three arrivals at one fix is evidence the fix is right and stronger evidence that a mechanism, not a habit, is what is missing
  sesa: the count is now three lanes and at least seven commits; a pre-commit hook refusing staged paths outside the committing lane's claim is the item, and it is the only one of the three sesa in 0013 that nobody has picked up
  pramana: machine/dosa.lekha, dosa 0009 and dosa 0013
  pramana: git-commit(1), --only
  pramana: AHIMSA_SUTRA_VISTARA §6 -- लिखितो दोषो जीवति । अलिखितो दोषो हिंसा । and §31, व्ययो वक्तव्यः तत्रैव: the cost is stated where it was incurred
  uttara: 0013
  sara: 81b339882dd8aae5
.
dosa 0015
  kala: 2026-08-20
  karta: cf-sabhapariksa
  jati: nasti-krta
  vastu: machine/Sabda_TheWireHasNoBoolean.hs:look
  yatna: Adversary pass over the wire the sabha talks on. The grammar refuses `true`, `false`, a float and `null`, each with a repair, and I confirmed all four refusals fire. So I attacked the ACCESSORS instead of the grammar: send a well-formed utterance whose object carries the same key twice.
  hetu: `J` holds an object as `JObj [(String, J)]` -- an ordered association list that PRESERVES repeats, deliberately, and DosaLekha_TheWrittenDefectRecord.hs states the reason in its own type ("A Map here would silently keep one witness and drop the rest -- which is the exact failure the file is about"). `look` then reads that list with `Data.List.lookup`, which returns the FIRST binding and discards the rest with no error and no residue. The parser preserves the multiplicity and the accessor destroys it, so the loss happens between two of this lane's own files rather than at either one's boundary. Transport is not possible for the discarded binding because by the time any handler runs it is not in the value any more; the only remaining road was the written defect, and none was written.
  nasta: The witness list the client actually sent. `{"kriya":"naya.sthapana","angani":{"naya":"X","saksin":["one"],"saksin":["two"]}}` installs `X` with `["one"]`. `["two"]` is destroyed. No dosa-lekha is emitted, the answer is a SAMKRAMANA, and its tulyata reads `saksin: stored verbatim: no normalisation, no deduplication, no reordering, so the list you get back is the list you sent`. The list you get back is not the list you sent. This is a transport whose exhibited equivalence is false, which is the third road under the name of the first.
  nasta: The vama field of that same tulyata reads `the 1 witness string(s) you sent`. Two were sent. The count is computed from the survivor, so the answer reports the size of what it kept as the size of what it received, and a reader auditing the transcript cannot recover the discrepancy from the answer -- only from the raw `prasna` line, which the answer never points at.
  nasta: The choice of operation itself. `{"kriya":"sabha.kriyah","kriya":"kuttaka","angani":{"a":137,"b":60}}` dispatches sabha.kriyah, runs it, and never mentions that a second operation was named in the same utterance. An assembly that silently picks one of two requested acts has issued a verdict with no witness that it chose.
  yogyata-drsta: Built machine/SabhaRun.hs with ghc -O0 -imachine and drove the produced binary on stdin with the three utterances above; read the emitted lines. Also read Sabda_TheWireHasNoBoolean.hs in full (233 lines) and confirmed `look`, `jStr`, `jStrs`, `jInt` and `jObj` all route through the same `lookup`.
  yogyata-ksetra: The five accessors exported by Sabda_TheWireHasNoBoolean and every call site of them in Sabha_TheSessionKernelAnLLMTalksTo.hs. Not searched: other importers of this module elsewhere in machine/.
  yogyata-avadhi: Says nothing about whether a duplicate key is reachable from any caller other than a hostile or careless client, and nothing about DosaLekha's own on-disk parser, which handles repeats correctly by keeping them (`fieldsNamed`).
  punarabhinaya: d=/tmp/sabha-adv-replay; mkdir -p $d; ghc -O0 -imachine -outputdir $d/o -o $d/sabha machine/SabhaRun.hs >/dev/null 2>&1; printf '{"kriya":"naya.sthapana","angani":{"naya":"X","saksin":["one"],"saksin":["two"]}}\n' | SABHA_LEKHA=$d/t.jsonl $d/sabha 2>/dev/null
  phala: out~the 1 witness string(s) you sent
  sesa: The repair is not "deduplicate". It is that `look` has the same two roads available to it as everything else: return the value when the key is bound once, and return a defect naming both bindings when it is bound twice. `look` already returns `Either String J` and already writes sentences into its Left.
  sesa: DosaLekha's parser got this right and Sabda's did not, in the same session, for the same reason, one directory apart. That is the transmission failure CLAUDE.md diagnoses -- the rule was not in hand at the moment of the act -- and the mechanism that would fire at the moment of the act is a property test asserting that `look k` on an object with a repeated key does not return a Right.
  pramana: notes/AHIMSA_SUTRA_VISTARA.md §6 -- तृतीयो मार्गो न विद्यते; and §32, सङ्क्षेपो नष्टिः
  pramana: machine/DosaLekha_TheWrittenDefectRecord.hs, the comment on `data Dosa` stating why repeats are preserved
  pramana: machine/GATE_AUDIT_DISPOSITION.md §2 -- the same shape: sound against its stated adversary, unsound against its environment
  sara: 97ceb42ace16c46a
.
dosa 0016
  kala: 2026-08-20
  karta: cf-sabhapariksa
  jati: nasti-krta
  vastu: machine/Sabha_TheSessionKernelAnLLMTalksTo.hs, `answer`, the `args` binding
  yatna: Following dosa 0015 down one layer: if the accessors collapse, does the dispatcher above them collapse too. It does, and by a different mechanism, so the two are separate defects.
  hetu: `answer` reads the argument object as `either (const (JObj [])) id (look "angani" j >>= \a -> jObj a >> Right a)`. Both failure modes of that expression -- the key is ABSENT, and the key is PRESENT AND NOT AN OBJECT -- are mapped to the same value, the empty object. The Left carrying which one it was is discarded by `const`. Transport is not possible after that point because the distinction is no longer in the value; the handler receives an empty object and can only report what an empty object supports, which is "no key `a`".
  nasta: The fact that an `angani` was sent at all. `{"kriya":"kuttaka","angani":[1,2,3]}` and `{"kriya":"kuttaka"}` produce BYTE-IDENTICAL answers, both reading `the request could not be read: no key `a` in the utterance; keys present: ` -- an empty key listing for an utterance that carried a three-element array. The client is told it omitted something it did not omit.
  nasta: This is exactly the distinction the same lane's own grammar refuses `null` in order to protect. Sabda_TheWireHasNoBoolean's parser answers `null` with "Absence here is said by omitting the key; a key present-and-null flattens `not given` and `given as nothing` into one." The parser refuses that flattening at the wire and the dispatcher performs it three lines into the handler, on the argument object, for every operation in the table.
  nasta: The Left sentence itself, which `look` and `jObj` had already composed and which said precisely which of the two it was. It is constructed and then thrown away by `const`, so the work of writing the defect was done and the writing was not.
  yogyata-drsta: Ran the two utterances above through the built sabha binary and diffed the two output lines; they are identical. Read `answer` and `logIf` in full.
  yogyata-ksetra: `answer` in Sabha_TheSessionKernelAnLLMTalksTo.hs, and the twelve handlers in `kriyah` that receive its `args`.
  yogyata-avadhi: Says nothing about the `kriya` key itself, which is read with `jStr` and whose failure IS reported (a separate branch, correctly). Only the `angani` binding is at fault.
  punarabhinaya: d=/tmp/sabha-adv-replay; mkdir -p $d; ghc -O0 -imachine -outputdir $d/o -o $d/sabha machine/SabhaRun.hs >/dev/null 2>&1; printf '{"kriya":"kuttaka","angani":[1,2,3]}\n{"kriya":"kuttaka"}\n' | SABHA_LEKHA=$d/t2.jsonl $d/sabha 2>/dev/null | sort -u | awk 'END{print (NR==1)?"IDENTICAL-ANSWERS":"DIFFERENT"}'
  phala: out~IDENTICAL-ANSWERS
  sesa: The type already carries the answer: `look "angani" j >>= jObj` is `Either String [(String,J)]`, and `answer` could pass the Left straight into `malformed`, which exists two functions away and is already the right shape.
  pramana: machine/Sabda_TheWireHasNoBoolean.hs, the refusal of `null` and its stated reason
  pramana: notes/AHIMSA_SUTRA_VISTARA.md §6
  sara: 4039fe5152ba8118
.
dosa 0017
  kala: 2026-08-20
  karta: cf-sabhapariksa
  jati: karana-dosa
  vastu: machine/Sabda_TheWireHasNoBoolean.hs `quote` and `pstring`; machine/Sabha_TheSessionKernelAnLLMTalksTo.hs `serve`
  yatna: Testing the founding claim directly: two roads and no third. If any input produces neither a samkramana nor a dosa-lekha, the claim is false. I looked for an input the wire's READER accepts and its WRITER cannot write.
  hetu: `pstring` implements the `\u` escape as `chr (foldl ...)` over any four hex digits, so it admits the surrogate range U+D800-U+DFFF. `quote` escapes control characters and passes everything else through raw; a surrogate is not `isControl`, so it is emitted raw. GHC's UTF-8 encoder throws on a surrogate. The exception is raised inside `hPutStrLn hout` in `serve`, which has no handler, so there is no point at which either road can be taken: the answer is already half on the wire when the process dies. Transport is impossible because the value cannot be encoded; a written defect is impossible because the writing is what throws.
  nasta: The third road, exhibited. `{"kriya":"naya.sthapana","angani":{"naya":"S","saksin":["\ud800"]}}` produces 378 bytes of a TRUNCATED samkramana on stdout, cut off inside the `saksinah` array, followed by process death with exit 1. The client receives a partial utterance that is neither road and is not even a complete JSON object. AHIMSA_SUTRA_VISTARA §6 states तृतीयो मार्गो न विद्यते. Here it exists and it is the default behaviour of the reader accepting what the writer cannot write.
  nasta: The entire session. `sSesa`, `sDosa` and `sNaya` live only in memory; `serve` calls `appendLekha` AFTER `hPutStrLn hout`, so the turn that killed the process is never written and neither is anything about the turns before it that had not yet been flushed. On a fresh session the transcript file is not even created: `ls $SABHA_LEKHA` reports No such file or directory. Every defect the interlocutor had written into the log with `dosa.lekha` in that session is destroyed, unwritten, by one four-character escape. §6: अलिखितो दोषो हिंसा.
  nasta: The invariant `selftest` checks. Its script drives 25 utterances and asserts every answer is one of the two roads, structurally, on the `Uttara` value -- which is sound, and which cannot see this, because the value IS well-formed and the loss happens in `render`'s encoder on the way out. The check is on the wrong side of the boundary: it inspects the answer, not the bytes the answer becomes.
  yogyata-drsta: Built machine/SabhaRun.hs, fed the utterance above on stdin with stdout and stderr redirected to files, and read all three of: the exit status (1), the byte count of stdout (378, truncated mid-object), and the absence of the transcript file. Stderr carries `GHC.Internal.IO.Encoding.Failure` with an ioException backtrace.
  yogyata-ksetra: `quote`/`esc` and `pstring`'s `\u` branch in Sabda_TheWireHasNoBoolean.hs, and `serve`/`appendLekha` in Sabha_TheSessionKernelAnLLMTalksTo.hs. Also checked `isControl`: in GHC it is generalCategory == Control, which excludes surrogates (they are `Surrogate`), so the existing escape branch does not cover them.
  yogyata-avadhi: Says nothing about whether the same escape reaches machine/DosaLekha's on-disk log, which has a different reader. Says nothing about paired surrogates, which I did not test. Says nothing about the same fault on the READ side under a non-UTF-8 ambient locale, which GATE_AUDIT_DISPOSITION.md §6 records as a separate live fault in a different lane.
  punarabhinaya: d=/tmp/sabha-adv-replay; mkdir -p $d; ghc -O0 -imachine -outputdir $d/o -o $d/sabha machine/SabhaRun.hs >/dev/null 2>&1; rm -f $d/t3.jsonl; printf '{"kriya":"naya.sthapana","angani":{"naya":"S","saksin":["\\ud800"]}}\n' | SABHA_LEKHA=$d/t3.jsonl $d/sabha >$d/o3.txt 2>/dev/null; echo "exit=$?"; wc -c < $d/o3.txt; ls $d/t3.jsonl 2>&1 | tail -1
  phala: out~No such file or directory
  sesa: Two independent repairs, and they are not alternatives. (1) `pstring` should refuse a lone surrogate the way it already refuses a `\u` without four hex digits -- the reader must not admit what the writer cannot write. (2) `serve` must not be able to die mid-line: the answer should be rendered to a String and the encoding failure caught BEFORE anything is put on the wire, so an unencodable answer becomes a dosa-lekha about itself rather than a truncation.
  sesa: The deeper item is the ordering in `serve`: the transcript is written after the answer is flushed, so the record of a turn is contingent on the turn surviving. Appending first would have made this defect self-recording.
  pramana: notes/AHIMSA_SUTRA_VISTARA.md §6 -- द्वौ मार्गौ, तृतीयो न विद्यते; and अलिखितो दोषो हिंसा
  pramana: machine/GATE_AUDIT_DISPOSITION.md §5 and §6 -- IO exceptions escaping and aborting the engine, and the locale fault's three mouths; this is a fourth mouth of the same family
  sara: 5e85bea0bdc9b94e
.
dosa 0018
  kala: 2026-08-20
  karta: cf-sabhapariksa
  jati: ayogya-darsana
  vastu: machine/DosaLekha_TheWrittenDefectRecord.hs, `validate` and `cmdReplay`
  yatna: Attacking the organ that holds the findings rather than the findings. The charge was: make the defect log accept a DESCRIBED loss instead of an EXHIBITED one. I wrote a complete defect record for an event that never happened and re-exhibited it successfully.
  hetu: The file's own required-field table says of `nasta`: "NO WITNESS: the loss is described instead of exhibited -- refused". The check implementing it is `length (trim w) < 12`. That is a character count, and this file's own answer to the same question, four fields above, is "a count is ∥·∥₁ of the list it replaces". Separately, `cmdReplay --run` executes `punarabhinaya` through `readCreateProcessWithExitCode (shell cmd)` and compares `phala: exit=<n>` against the exit status of that shell command. The exit status of a pipeline is its last stage's. Transport of the distinction "this loss was exhibited" is not possible through a length, and transport of "this defect still occurs" is not possible through a pipeline's exit status; both are structurally unavailable, so the defect is written.
  nasta: `nasta: aaaaaaaaaaaa` -- twelve characters, no content -- is accepted, and so is `nasta: some things were lost`, which is a description in the file's own sense. The distinction between an exhibited loss (a path:line, a command with its output, a pair of log lines four apart, which is what dosa 0001 through 0014 all carry) and a sentence about a loss is not held anywhere in the record, in the validator, or in the chain. The requirement is stated in prose and enforced by strlen.
  nasta: `punarabhinaya: false 2>&1 | cat` with `phala: exit=0` replays as `-- exit 0` and prints `-- REPLAYED: got the recorded exit 0.  The defect is still exhibited.` The command is `false`. Nothing was exhibited. This is machine/GATE_AUDIT_DISPOSITION.md §2 verbatim -- "the cheapest is not exotic: `agda "$@" 2>&1 | cat` ... The exit status of a pipeline is its last stage's" -- reappearing in a lane written after the disposition, against a different kernel, with no repair carried across. The repair exists in this repository and did not travel.
  nasta: The whole of re-exhibition as a pramana. A record whose replay reports REPLAYED is read by every later agent as evidence the defect is live; `cmdCount` and `cmdQuery` present all records alike. A record that can manufacture that verdict makes the verdict worthless for the fourteen honest records it sits beside, which is the cost the fabrication imposes on work that was done correctly.
  yogyata-drsta: Wrote the fabricated entry through `sh machine/run-dosa-lekha.sh write` into a scratch log at $DOSA_LEKHA and it was ACCEPTED (exit 0, dosa 0001 appended); then ran `replay 1 --run` on it and read the REPLAYED line. Separately confirmed the validator is not merely permissive: an entry missing yogyata-drsta, yogyata-ksetra, yogyata-avadhi and punarabhinaya, with `nasta: short`, was refused with all five reasons named, exit 1. So the gate rejects an incomplete record and accepts a complete false one, which is the GateAudit shape exactly.
  yogyata-ksetra: `required`, `validate`, `cmdWrite`, `cmdReplay` and `readMaybeInt` in DosaLekha_TheWrittenDefectRecord.hs, read in full; and the fourteen records standing in machine/dosa.lekha, checked for what their own `nasta` fields actually carry.
  yogyata-avadhi: Says nothing about the `out~` form of `phala`, which I did not attack but which is trivially satisfiable by `echo`. Says nothing about whether any existing record in machine/dosa.lekha is fabricated -- I read them and they are not; the finding is about what the gate permits, not about what is in the log.
  punarabhinaya: d=/tmp/sabha-adv-replay; mkdir -p $d; rm -f $d/fab.lekha; printf 'kala: 2026-08-20\nkarta: adversary\njati: nasti-krta\nyatna: fabricating a defect that never happened\nhetu: nothing failed; this record is invented end to end\nnasta: aaaaaaaaaaaa\nyogyata-drsta: nothing was looked at\nyogyata-ksetra: nowhere\nyogyata-avadhi: none\npunarabhinaya: false 2>&1 | cat\nphala: exit=0\n' | DOSA_LEKHA=$d/fab.lekha sh machine/run-dosa-lekha.sh write >/dev/null && DOSA_LEKHA=$d/fab.lekha sh machine/run-dosa-lekha.sh replay 1 --run | tail -1
  phala: out~The defect is still exhibited
  sesa: The replay repair is the one GATE_AUDIT_DISPOSITION.md §2 already made and stated as a general principle: no acceptance is honoured by a process that has not watched its own kernel reject a falsehood. Here that means `replay --run` needs a canary of its own -- a command known to fail and a command known to succeed, both run in the same shell, both uncached -- before any REPLAYED is printed. And the captured output should be scanned, not only the status.
  sesa: The `nasta` repair is not a longer minimum. A count cannot distinguish exhibition from description at any threshold. What distinguishes them is that an exhibit is CHECKABLE: a path that exists, a path:line whose line exists, a command whose output is quoted, a pair of quoted strings. `validate` could require that at least one `nasta` in each record contains something the machine can go and look at, and say so when it cannot -- which is the same demand `yogyata-drsta` already makes of the looking.
  sesa: Neither repair is mine to make; both belong to the lane that owns this file, and this record is the pramana for them.
  pramana: machine/GATE_AUDIT_DISPOSITION.md §2 -- "A gate that cannot be fooled by a false statement but can be fooled by a shell wrapper is not a gate"
  pramana: machine/DosaLekha_TheWrittenDefectRecord.hs, `required`, the entry for `nasta`
  pramana: notes/AHIMSA_SUTRA_VISTARA.md §19 -- यत् अनङ्गीकृतमार्गेण आगच्छति तत् न दुर्बलं प्रमाणम् । तत् अप्रमाणम् ।
  sara: f540fe3574cf7fc5
.
dosa 0019
  kala: 2026-08-20
  karta: cf-sabhapariksa
  jati: nasti-krta
  vastu: machine/DosaLekha_TheWrittenDefectRecord.hs, `parseLog`.`body` and `checkChain`
  yatna: The charge said: forge a chain link. I did not need to compute a hash. `dosalekha verify` reports "chain intact, nothing edited or deleted" over a file into which I injected an arbitrary line of text inside an existing record.
  hetu: The chain hashes `canonical i fs`, the text REBUILT from the parsed fields, not the bytes on disk. `parseLog`'s record body discards two things before the fields are formed: any line whose trimmed form begins with `#`, and all leading and trailing whitespace on every key and value (`trim k`, `trim v`, and `trim (drop 1 (trim l))` on a `|` continuation). Those discarded things are therefore outside the chain entirely. Transport is not possible: FNV-1a over a projection cannot witness anything the projection forgets, and this is not a weakness of FNV-1a -- SHA-256 in the same place would have the same hole.
  nasta: The chain's claim about the file. `sed '20i\  # SUPERSEDED -- this defect was withdrawn; ignore it.' machine/dosa.lekha > forged.lekha` inserts that sentence in the middle of dosa 0001's `nasta` block, and `dosalekha verify` on the result prints `14 records, chain intact, nothing edited or deleted.` and exits 0. The channel is unbounded: any amount of arbitrary text, anywhere in the log, at any indentation, is free.
  nasta: The distinction between the file a human reads and the object the chain covers. Every reader of this log -- `cat`, `git diff`, an editor, an agent grepping it -- reads the bytes. `verify` attests to the parse. The header written into the top of the log says "Edit any line above the last record and `dosalekha verify` names the record where it happened." That sentence is false for the two classes above, and it is the sentence a later agent will rely on.
  nasta: What a `#` line can be made to say. It sits inside the record, at the record's own indentation, in the middle of its exhibits; a reader has no way to tell it apart from the record's content except by knowing that `#` is a comment marker in a format whose comment marker is documented nowhere in the file it produces. The injected line in the replay says the record was withdrawn.
  yogyata-drsta: Ran the sed above against the committed machine/dosa.lekha, read lines 17-23 of the result to confirm the injected line sits inside dosa 0001's nasta block, then ran `DOSA_LEKHA=... verify` and read its output and exit status. Separately confirmed the chain is NOT vacuous: changing one character of a value (`karta: nasti-lane` to `karta: someone-else`) breaks it, correctly, naming dosa 0014 and line 318, and `write` then refuses to append.
  yogyata-ksetra: `parseLog`, `canonical`, `fnv1a`, `saraOf`, `checkChain`, `renderDosa`, `cmdVerify`, `cmdWrite` and `parseEntry` in DosaLekha_TheWrittenDefectRecord.hs, all read in full.
  yogyata-avadhi: Says nothing about deleting or altering a FIELD, which does break the chain (checked). Says nothing about recomputing the chain outright, which is trivially possible because FNV-1a is unkeyed -- the file states that adversary is out of scope and I accept the statement; this finding is inside the scope it claims, because it does not recompute anything.
  punarabhinaya: d=/tmp/sabha-adv-replay; mkdir -p $d; sed '20i\
  phala: out~chain intact, nothing edited or deleted
  sesa: The honest statement `verify` can make today is "the fields of these records recompute to their recorded sara". It is currently making the stronger statement "nothing edited or deleted", and the gap between the two is the finding.
  sesa: The repair that closes it, if the lane wants the stronger statement, is to hash the record's BYTES between its header and its terminator rather than the canonical rebuild -- which costs the property that `query` output can be piped back into `write`, so it is a real trade and not an oversight to be scolded for. The cheaper repair is to make `canonical` carry the comment lines and the raw indentation, so the projection stops forgetting.
  pramana: machine/DosaLekha_TheWrittenDefectRecord.hs §3, "Not cryptographic and not claimed to be: the adversary here is an accidental edit, a lost rebase, a truncating write" -- an accidental edit that ADDS a line is inside that stated adversary and is not caught
  pramana: notes/AHIMSA_SUTRA_VISTARA.md §31 -- यत् सङ्क्रम्यते तत् किञ्चित् त्यजति । यो न वदति स न पश्यति ।
  sara: 4ed90f67a42ae017
.
dosa 0020
  kala: 2026-08-20
  karta: cf-sabhapariksa
  jati: karana-dosa
  vastu: machine/dosa.lekha, dosa 0019, its `punarabhinaya` field
  uttara: 0019
  yatna: Repairing my own record. Dosa 0019's replay command was written as a `sed '20i\' ... ` insert, whose syntax requires an embedded newline. A multi-line value in this format is stored as a `|` continuation and the continuation's content is TRIMMED, so the two leading spaces of the injected line were lost and sed exited 2. `replay 19 --run` therefore DIVERGES, and the finding it records is nevertheless real.
  hetu: A record is never edited here, so the wrong command in 0019 cannot be corrected in place; §6's second road is the only one available and this is it. The finding of 0019 stands unchanged: `parseLog` discards `#` lines and all leading/trailing whitespace before the chain is computed, so both are outside it. The `punarabhinaya` of 0019 was an instrument defect, not a defect in the finding, which is why this record's jati is karana-dosa and not a retraction.
  nasta: The re-exhibition of dosa 0019, which was unavailable from the moment it was written until this record. A defect nobody can re-exhibit is what the `punarabhinaya` field exists to prevent, and my own record failed the requirement it was written to enforce, in the same session in which I found that the requirement can be satisfied by a command that exhibits nothing (dosa 0018). Both failures are about the same thing: the field is checked for presence, never for whether it runs.
  nasta: The trimming of continuation content is itself a small instance of 0019's finding, discovered by being bitten by it: `body`'s `|` branch computes `v ++ "\n" ++ trim (drop 1 (trim l))`, so a continuation line cannot carry leading whitespace, and any value whose second line is indented is silently reindented on the way in. That is a third class of byte the chain does not cover, alongside comment lines and outer whitespace, and 0019 named only the first two.
  yogyata-drsta: Ran `sh machine/run-dosa-lekha.sh replay 19 --run` and read `-- exit 2` and the DIVERGED verdict; then rewrote the insert with awk, ran it against the live machine/dosa.lekha at 19 records, read lines 18-22 of the output to confirm the injected line sits inside dosa 0001, and read `verify`'s output.
  yogyata-ksetra: dosa 0019 as stored; `cmdReplay` and `parseLog`.`body` in machine/DosaLekha_TheWrittenDefectRecord.hs.
  yogyata-avadhi: Says nothing about whether any other record in this log has a punarabhinaya that no longer runs; I checked 0015 through 0018, which do, and did not run 0001 through 0014.
  punarabhinaya: d=/tmp/sabha-adv-replay; mkdir -p $d; awk 'NR==20{print "  # SUPERSEDED -- this defect was withdrawn; ignore it.  (injected, and not hashed)"}1' machine/dosa.lekha > $d/forged.lekha; DOSA_LEKHA=$d/forged.lekha sh machine/run-dosa-lekha.sh verify
  phala: out~chain intact, nothing edited or deleted
  sesa: `dosalekha write` could run the `punarabhinaya` it is being handed and compare against `phala` before accepting the record, refusing an entry whose replay does not reproduce what it claims. That would have caught 0019 at the moment of the act and it does not touch the harder question 0018 raises, which is whether the command exhibits anything at all.
  pramana: machine/dosa.lekha dosa 0019, and dosa 0018 §sesa
  pramana: notes/AHIMSA_SUTRA_VISTARA.md §6 -- a record is corrected by appending, never by editing
  sara: 2086a0f8f89d5bdb
.
dosa 0021
  kala: 2026-08-20
  karta: cf-sabhapariksa
  jati: ayogya-darsana
  vastu: machine/NayaKosha_TheStandpointStore.hs, `satya` and `decide`
  yatna: The charge was: make an unfit looking issue a verdict, and get two standpoints merged without an equivalence. Both, in one entry, against the module that exists to prevent exactly these.
  hetu: `satya e | not (null (entWitness e)) = Just True | Yogya _ <- entYogyata e = Just False | otherwise = Nothing`. Yogyata is consulted only in the branch where the witness list is EMPTY. Kumarila's condition is therefore applied to absence and never to presence: a standpoint whose looking is declared `Ayogya` but which nevertheless carries witnesses gets `Just True` and passes into `fit` unremarked. Transport of the fitness into the verdict is not possible because `decide` partitions on `satya`, which has already discarded it -- the information is in the Entry, is never read, and by the time the bhanga is chosen there is nothing to carry.
  nasta: The declared unfitness of a standpoint. Two entries with identical witnesses, one `Yogya "exhaustive grep over notes/, 2026-08-20"` and one `Ayogya "I did not look at all; these are invented"`, produce `Position B1Asti`, `samata = Just MulaSama`, `nirShesha = []`, `nirNashti = []`, and the sentence "IDENTIFIABLE, at every index this store holds. Same labels, same sources. Holding both said one thing twice." The invented standpoint is identified with the searched one, at the strongest level the store has, and the string saying it was invented appears nowhere in the answer. The module's own widening 1 says "each entry carries the fitness of the looking that produced its witnesses"; it carries it and does not read it.
  nasta: `entRecorder` and `entWhen`. The header defines `entMula` as "where the ENTRY came from (who recorded it, when, out of what) as distinct from where its witnesses came from" -- and then no index uses either field, no loss report mentions either, and `nirMulaLoss` is computed only at ArthaSama so it is empty at MulaSama by construction. In the run above the two entries differ in recorder ("cf-honest" against "adversary") and the verdict is B1Asti with both loss lists empty. The module's whole THE INDEX argument is that independent attestation of one content must not be collapsed; it holds that line at the witness level, defines the fields to hold it at the entry level, and drops it there with no defect written. D1 through D5 do not name this.
  nasta: The `Yogya` reason string, which is required by the type and checked by nothing. `Yogya ""` is a well-typed fitness claim with no account of the search, and the header says "a fitness claim with no account of the search is itself unfit". The type carries the obligation; nothing enforces it.
  yogyata-drsta: Built a driver against machine/NayaKosha_TheStandpointStore.hs with ghc -e and read the printed `satya e2 = Just True`, the sthana, the samata, and the two empty loss lists. Read `satya`, `artha`, `mula`, `decide`, `insert` and the D1-D5 defect list in full.
  yogyata-ksetra: NayaKosha_TheStandpointStore.hs, all 672 lines. Also checked machine/Sabha_TheSessionKernelAnLLMTalksTo.hs, which does NOT use this store -- it dispatches into machine/Naya.hs with bare (name, [witness]) pairs and has no yogyata input at all, so the defect is not reachable through the wire today.
  yogyata-avadhi: Says nothing about `insert`, which I did not attack and which does look correct -- it stores duplicates, reports every relation independently rather than as a first-match chain, and never merges. The finding is confined to `satya` and to what `decide` does not carry.
  punarabhinaya: ghc -O0 -imachine -e 'let e1 = Entry 0 "honest" (Yogya "exhaustive grep, 2026-08-20") "cf-honest" "2026-08-20" [Sakshin "Wehr 1987" "PMID 3651732"]; e2 = Entry 1 "guessed" (Ayogya "I did not look at all; invented") "adversary" "2026-08-20" [Sakshin "Wehr 1987" "PMID 3651732"] in case decide True [e1,e2] of { Abhinna m -> putStrLn ("ABHINNA " ++ m); n -> putStrLn ("VERDICT " ++ show (nirSthana n) ++ " samata=" ++ show (nirSamata n) ++ " shesha=" ++ show (nirShesha n) ++ " nashti=" ++ show (nirNashti n)) }' machine/NayaKosha_TheStandpointStore.hs 2>/dev/null
  phala: out~VERDICT Position B1Asti samata=Just MulaSama shesha=[] nashti=[]
  sesa: `satya` cannot answer this alone -- an Ayogya entry with witnesses is a genuinely third case, not a True and not a False and not the silence the module already has a slot for. It is the residue: a looking declared unfit says nothing about the object whatever it brought back, so the entry belongs in `nirShesha` with its reason, which is the machinery the module already built for the empty case.
  sesa: The entry-level provenance is the harder item and it is the one worth more. Either `entRecorder`/`entWhen` become a fourth index above mula -- call it what the tradition calls it, not what I would call it -- or their absence from every index becomes D6, written. The header's own standard is that a quotient which is kept must be noted; this one is kept and not noted.
  pramana: Kumarila Bhatta, Slokavarttika, Abhavapariccheda, c. 7th c. -- yogya-anupalabdhi: "not seen" is not a pramana, "not seen where it would be seen" is; the condition is on the LOOKING and not on what the looking returned
  pramana: machine/NayaKosha_TheStandpointStore.hs, THE INDEX, and its own remark that Naya.hs "cites Kumarila ... and then the code does the thing the citation forbids"
  pramana: formal/cubical/NaturalMachine/Durnaya_CollapseIffEveryNayaAgrees.agda
  sara: c2b17a42ef74225e
.
dosa 0022
  kala: 2026-08-20
  karta: cf-sabhapariksa
  jati: sankramana-asambhava
  vastu: machine/Uttara_SamkramanaOrDosalekhaNeverABareBoolean.hs, `Tulyata`.`tuWitness` and `samkramana`
  yatna: The general statement the other six records are instances of. I looked for the place where a SAMKRAMANA is checked against the equivalence it claims to have moved along, and there is no such place.
  hetu: `Tulyata` is four Strings. `samkramana` refuses a transport whose `tuWitness` is EMPTY, and cannot refuse one whose `tuWitness` is FALSE, because a String has no relation to the objects named in `tuLeft` and `tuRight` that any code here can examine. Transport is not possible for the property "this identification holds": the type carries prose where the sutra's §6 carries `ua e`, and prose is not an equivalence. Voevodsky's point is quoted correctly in the header -- "the identification is a thing you hold, not a fact you cite" -- and the field holds a citation.
  nasta: The exhibited-ness of every saksin field in every answer this machine gives. GATE_AUDIT_DISPOSITION.md §2 states the discipline: "no acceptance is honoured by a process that has not watched its own kernel reject a falsehood", and pairs canaryTrue with canaryFalse so the kernel is watched failing. Nothing in this lane is ever watched failing a tulyata. `selftest` drives 25 utterances and checks that `tuWitness` is non-empty; it never checks that any one of them is true, and there is no construction anywhere in the lane whose tulyata is meant to be rejected.
  nasta: A live instance, in the lane's own dispatcher, reachable in one utterance. `{"kriya":"dosa.lekha","angani":{"kriya":"transporting X","hetu":"no equivalence","nasta":[]}}` returns a SAMKRAMANA whose tulyata reads `stored verbatim: no normalisation, no rewording, no summarising; the entry below is the entry`, and the entry below has been reworded: the client's hetu `no equivalence` is stored as ``a defect was logged for `transporting X` naming nothing lost: no equivalence``, and a nasta the client never wrote has been inserted. The rewording is CORRECT -- `Uttara`'s own smart constructor degrades an empty defect into a defect about itself, which is the right behaviour. The falsehood is the transport's witness asserting there was none.
  nasta: Two further instances already written, so the count is at least three and the shape is one. Dosa 0015: the tulyata reads `no deduplication ... the list you get back is the list you sent` while a duplicate key destroyed half the list. Dosa 0017: the tulyata is never delivered at all, because the encoder dies mid-line, and the truncation is neither road. Three unrelated causes, one unchecked field, no mechanism in between.
  yogyata-drsta: Read Uttara_SamkramanaOrDosalekhaNeverABareBoolean.hs in full (207 lines) and grepped its three constructors' call sites in machine/Sabha_TheSessionKernelAnLLMTalksTo.hs; ran the dosa.lekha utterance above through the built sabha and read the returned object.
  yogyata-ksetra: The `Uttara` and `Tulyata` types, `samkramana`, `dosalekha`, `raw`, `uttaraJ`, `uttaraLines`, the `Nivedaka` class, and `selftest`'s invariant block in the sabha kernel.
  yogyata-avadhi: Says nothing about whether a checkable tulyata is achievable in Haskell at all -- it plainly is not in general, since the two sides are arbitrary objects. The finding is that there is no case in this lane where a tulyata IS checkable and is checked, and that the arithmetic ones (kuttaka's `a*x + b*y = g`, vargaprakrti's `a^2 - D*b^2 = k`) are exactly such cases: the witness strings are built by computing the identity and then rendering it, so the computation is present and its result is never compared to what it should be.
  punarabhinaya: d=/tmp/sabha-adv-replay; mkdir -p $d; ghc -O0 -imachine -outputdir $d/o -o $d/sabha machine/SabhaRun.hs >/dev/null 2>&1; printf '{"kriya":"dosa.lekha","angani":{"kriya":"transporting X","hetu":"no equivalence","nasta":[]}}\n' | SABHA_LEKHA=$d/t8.jsonl $d/sabha 2>/dev/null
  phala: out~naming nothing lost
  sesa: The tractable half is real and is not a rewrite. Where a tulyata's witness is an arithmetic identity the handler already computed, the handler can CHECK it and emit a dosa-lekha when it fails -- and the lane can then do what GateAudit's repair did, and run a deliberately false tulyata through the same path once per process, so that no samkramana is honoured by a process that has not watched one rejected. kuttaka and vargaprakrti both already compute the quantity; vargaprakrti's own witness string even prints `a^2 - D*b^2 = <computed>` and does not compare it to `k`.
  sesa: The intractable half should be said rather than closed: a tulyata over two prose descriptions is a claim by the author of the handler, not an exhibit, and the type could distinguish the two -- a witness that was computed from a witness that was written -- which is the same distinction dosa 0018 finds missing in `nasta`. Two lanes, one missing distinction: exhibited against described.
  pramana: notes/AHIMSA_SUTRA_VISTARA.md §6 -- संक्रमणम् e = transport (ua e); संक्रमणे न किञ्चिन् नश्यति
  pramana: machine/GATE_AUDIT_DISPOSITION.md §2 -- canaryTrue must check, canaryFalse must not, both uncached per process
  pramana: machine/dosa.lekha, dosa 0015, 0017 and 0018
  sara: 0ba9ff57a8b1dc4f
.
dosa 0023
  kala: 2026-08-20
  karta: cf-samkramana-akanksa
  jati: nasti-krta
  vastu: machine/PairVocab.hs:264-268 and 662-666, machine/ArithVocab.hs:191-200 -- the evaluators of 0, s, +, *, - ; and machine/MathMachine.hs:721-738, read but not runnable from outside
  yatna: transport a symbol's declared expectancy (symArity, a number in the record) into the thing that consumes its arguments (symSem, a function), so that a caller offering the wrong number of arguments meets a refusal rather than an answer
  hetu: there is nothing to transport it along. symArity and symSem are two independent fields of `Sym` and no function anywhere in the three vocabulary modules relates them. An evaluator written `\vs -> vs!!0 + vs!!1` reads a prefix and never looks at the length, so lists of different lengths are identified by the accident that (!!) does not measure its argument; the identification is exhibited nowhere and no caller can recover which list arrived.
  hetu: eleven symbols in the same two modules -- gcd, mod, lcm, v2, leglo, leghi, splitnorm, nrm2, bcx1, bcx2, bcy -- are written as exhaustive matches and refuse at every other length. So the engine does not have one convention here, it has two, and which one a symbol received is recorded in no field and was decided by whoever typed the lambda.
  nasta: PairVocab `0` (arity 0) answered at offered lengths 1,2,3,4,5,6 -- on [7] it returned 0, the same value the empty list gives; one argument dropped, and six lists identified with the empty one.
  nasta: PairVocab `s` (arity 1) on [7,11] returned 8, and on [7,11,13,17,19,23] returned 8 -- the value [7] gives; five distinct lists identified with [7].
  nasta: PairVocab `+` (arity 2) on [7,11,13] returned 18 -- the value [7,11] gives; four lists identified with [7,11]. `*` on [7,11,13] returned 77. `-` on [7,11,13] returned 0.
  nasta: ArithVocab `0`, `s`, `+`, `*`, `-`: identically the same five symbols, the same lengths, the same values, because the two modules' base evaluators are copies of each other.
  nasta: machine/MathMachine.hs:909 -- `Nothing -> error ("MathMachine.eval: unknown symbol " ++ show f)`, with the comment "Unknown syntax must never acquire the accidental meaning zero." Four lines above it at :721, `Sym "0" 0 (const 0) []` gives an argument list of ANY length the accidental meaning zero. The fail-loud boundary was built for the name and not for the expectancy.
  yogyata-drsta: Ran every symSem in PairVocab.natVocabulary (12 symbols) and ArithVocab.vocabulary (9 symbols) at offered lengths 0..6 under `try . evaluate`, with distinct primes 7,11,13,17,19,23 as arguments so that the returned value names which prefix was read. Output is machine/AkanksaRun.hs sections 1 and 2, one Uttara per symbol.
  yogyata-ksetra: the two vocabulary modules that can be imported. MathMachine's own baseVocabulary is `module Main` and reachable by no import, so it was READ and not run; its `s` is written `\vs -> case vs of [v] -> v+1; _ -> error "successor received wrong arity"` where both copies are `\vs -> head vs + 1`, which is a third divergence between things called verbatim copies of each other.
  yogyata-avadhi: NO WRONG ANSWER IS CLAIMED. Term generation in the engine is arity-respecting and no caller is known today to offer a mis-lengthed list, so this is a latent identification and not a live bug; asserting otherwise would be the fitted-constant error in another costume. Says nothing about lengths above 6, nothing about argument values off {7,11,13,17,19,23}, and nothing about MathMachine's copy, which was not run.
  sesa: write the five base evaluators as exhaustive matches on the declared length, as the other eleven symbols in the same modules already are. That is the transport; it costs one line each.
  sesa: or make symArity derivable from symSem so the two fields cannot part -- the stronger repair, and the one that would also cover symbols added later, which the first does not.
  sesa: move baseVocabulary out of `module Main` so the engine's own copy can be compared at all. Today the three copies of the base vocabulary are checked against each other by prose in three headers.
  pramana: Sabara, Sabarabhasya on Jaimini's Mimamsasutra, c. 5th c. CE -- sentence-unity from mutual expectancy (akanksa)
  pramana: Visvanatha Nyayapancanana, Bhasapariccheda with Siddhantamuktavali, 17th c. -- akanksa, yogyata, sannidhi as the three conditions of sabdabodha; supply that does not match the expectancy yields no meaning, not a weaker one
  pramana: notes/AHIMSA_SUTRA_VISTARA.md sections 5 and 6 -- no retraction from a truncation; two roads, no third
  pramana: machine/Akanksa_TheDeclaredExpectancyAndTheEvaluatorAreIdentifiedByNothing.hs
  punarabhinaya: runghc -imachine machine/AkanksaRun.hs 2>/dev/null | grep -c 'argument lists of different lengths are identified'
  phala: out~10
  sara: 2c33fb7a824dbdee
.
dosa 0024
  kala: 2026-08-20
  karta: cf-matra
  jati: karana-dosa
  vastu: machine/KernelProbe.hs:52 `cubicalModule`, and its cancellation in scripts/Anuvrtti_TheOptionsLineIsSaidOnceAndContinues.sh
  yatna: CERTIFICATE_REACH.md 10.6 names `sh machine/run-kernel-probe.sh` as the authority on whether the emitters' OPTIONS line suits the installed cubical library, and the Anuvrtti check closes by deferring to it in the same words. Before measuring the loop on this container I ran that authority. It says FAIL. I then checked whether the kernel it grades is actually incapable.
  hetu: There is no equivalence to transport along, because the two sides grade different objects. The probe's verdict is a fact about the flag set `--cubical --safe`; the claim its callers read off it is a fact about the kernel. On Agda 2.8.0 with a cubical library compiled under --guardedness, [InfectiveImport] makes the probe's own module unsatisfiable by construction, so no run of it can ever return OK on this container and its FAIL carries no information about the kernel whatever.
  nasta: `KERNEL-PROBE agda=2.8.0 refl=OK cubical=FAIL` -- printed by sh machine/run-kernel-probe.sh at HEAD a8b6435a, 2026-08-20, on this container.
  nasta: The kernel it graded FAIL, checking the engine's ACTUAL emitted OPTIONS line, byte for byte from Certificate.kOptionsPragma: module Probe3 with `{-# OPTIONS --cubical --guardedness --safe --no-import-sorts #-}`, `open import Cubical.Foundations.Prelude`, `probe : 2 + 2 = 4`, `probe = refl` -- agda exit = 0. The same file with only the OPTIONS line changed to the probe's `{-# OPTIONS --cubical --safe #-}` -- agda exit = 42.
  nasta: agda's diagnostic on the probe's own module, verbatim: `Probe2.agda:3.1-40: error: [InfectiveImport] / Importing module Cubical.Foundations.Prelude using the --guardedness flag from a module which does not. / when scope checking the declaration / open import Cubical.Foundations.Prelude`. This is the identical defect CERTIFICATE_REACH.md 10.1 diagnosed and 10.3 repaired in six other emitters; KernelProbe.hs is the one file exempted from the repair.
  nasta: The exemption's stated reason, from scripts/Anuvrtti_TheOptionsLineIsSaidOnceAndContinues.sh, which the run above prints: `its whole job is to answer whether THIS container checks a plain --cubical --safe module against the registered library. Pinning it to the flag set the emitters use would make it answer a different question than the one it reports.` The reason was true on Agda 2.6.3 and is what makes the probe uninformative on 2.8.0: the question it insists on asking is one this container answers FAIL to for every module ever written, including correct ones.
  nasta: The probe exits 0 regardless -- `exitWith (if reflOk then ExitSuccess else ExitFailure 2)` -- so no caller is stopped, no check goes red, and the miscalibration is visible only to a reader of the verdict line. CERTIFICATE_REACH.md 10.6's replay block lists the probe among the standing checks with no expected output recorded, so a FAIL there reads as ordinary.
  yogyata-drsta: Ran sh machine/run-kernel-probe.sh at HEAD a8b6435a; read machine/KernelProbe.hs in full (120 lines) including its 2026-08-19 appendix; ran the two probe modules directly under agda in a private mktemp directory and read both diagnostics and both true exit codes (42 and 0, taken from agda and not from a pipeline); ran scripts/Anuvrtti_TheOptionsLineIsSaidOnceAndContinues.sh and read its cancellation list; read CERTIFICATE_REACH.md section 10 in full.
  yogyata-ksetra: The two probe modules in machine/KernelProbe.hs, the OPTIONS literal in Certificate.kOptionsPragma, the cancellation list in scripts/Anuvrtti_TheOptionsLineIsSaidOnceAndContinues.sh, and the installed cubical library as registered for this container's agda 2.8.0.
  yogyata-avadhi: One container, one day, one agda (2.8.0). NO verdict on Agda 2.6.3, where the probe's question is satisfiable and the probe is correct -- this is a drift, not an original error, and the file's author had the right instrument for the container it was written against. NO verdict on whether the cubical registration is otherwise sound: the probe grades capability, not soundness, and so does this entry. NO verdict on the repair; changing the probe's flags would make it agree with the emitters and stop it being an independent control, which is the tension the exemption was protecting and which this entry does not resolve.
  punarabhinaya: sh machine/run-kernel-probe.sh; d=$(mktemp -d); cd $d; printf '{-# OPTIONS --cubical --safe #-}\nmodule Probe2 where\nopen import Cubical.Foundations.Prelude\nopen import Agda.Builtin.Nat\nprobe : 2 + 2 \xe2\x89\xa1 4\nprobe = refl\n' > Probe2.agda; sed 's/--cubical --safe/--cubical --guardedness --safe --no-import-sorts/; s/Probe2/Probe3/' Probe2.agda > Probe3.agda; agda -i . Probe2.agda >/dev/null 2>&1; echo "Probe2 exit=$?"; agda -i . Probe3.agda >/dev/null 2>&1; echo "Probe3 exit=$?"
  phala: out~cubical=FAIL
  sesa: The repair is not a flag change and that is the point. A control that is pinned to the emitters' flags stops being independent of them -- it would then answer `do the emitters agree with themselves`, which Anuvrtti already answers. What is actually wanted is a THIRD grade: the probe reports capability per flag set, and a container where `--cubical --safe` fails and the emitters' line succeeds is neither OK nor FAIL but a fact about the library's own compilation, which is what [InfectiveImport] is telling it. The verdict line has room: `cubical=<OK|FAIL|INFECTIVE>`.
  sesa: The deeper item, which is not this lane's: the probe's appendix proves in Agda that the map to its verdict is two-to-one over `false` -- absent and incapable share a grade -- and records that as a deliberate, harmless collapse because a guard consumes only the true fiber. This entry is a third inhabitant of that false fiber, and it is not harmless: `incapable` and `asking an unsatisfiable question` are not the same state, and the proof that the collapse loses nothing was a proof about the two inhabitants the author enumerated. The Agda file is true; its adequacy claim is what moved.
  sesa: machine/YantraSthiti_...sh section 6 currently reports `Toolchain: agda-2.6.3 (pin: Agda 2.8.0 ...)` and `NaturalMachine.agda exit: 42` -- exit 42 being the same code the probe's module returns here. Whether those are the same defect at a larger scale is not established by this entry and is the obvious next looking.
  pramana: Kumarila Bhatta, Slokavarttika, Abhavapariccheda, c. 7th c. -- yogya-anupalabdhi: an absence is a pramana only where the looking was FIT. The probe's looking is unfit on this container, so its FAIL is not evidence of incapacity; it is not evidence of anything.
  pramana: machine/CERTIFICATE_REACH.md section 10.1 and 10.3 -- the same [InfectiveImport] defect, diagnosed and repaired in six emitters, with KernelProbe.hs exempted by name
  pramana: notes/AHIMSA_SUTRA_VISTARA.md section 8 -- yat simam vadati tat na sanksipati: what states its limit does not collapse; and section 19 -- yat ananglkrtamargena agacchati tat na durbalam pramanam, tat apramanam
  sara: 72312565d9a7bb0a
.
dosa 0025
  kala: 2026-08-20
  karta: cf-samkramana-akanksa
  jati: upadhi-anaviskrta
  vastu: machine/ArithVocab.hs:151 and machine/PairVocab.hs:180 -- the two headers asserting that their base vocabulary is MathMachine's; and machine/MathMachine.hs:785 asserting fromAV is a relabelling
  yatna: identify the three copies of the base arithmetic vocabulary as one vocabulary, which is what all three headers say in prose and what `fromAV`/`avSym` do in code when ArithVocab's symbols are appended to MathMachine's list
  hetu: the identification was made on two of a Sym's four fields. `Sym` is name, arity, evaluator AND symDefs -- the defining equations the proof kernel is allowed to use -- and the defeating condition was in the fourth field, which nobody compared. ArithVocab.hs:151 says "Copied verbatim from MathMachine.hs so this module's terms ARE its terms"; PairVocab.hs:180 says its evaluators are "MathMachine's, verbatim ... checking the chart against the engine's own arithmetic and not against a second copy of it". Run against each other, the two are not each other's.
  nasta: `+` -- ArithVocab gives the kernel 2 equations, (x+0) -> x and (x+s(y)) -> s((x+y)); PairVocab gives it 0, none at all.
  nasta: `*` -- ArithVocab gives 2, (x*0) -> 0 and (x*s(y)) -> ((x*y)+x); PairVocab gives 0.
  nasta: `-` -- ArithVocab gives 3, -(x,0) -> x, -(0,x) -> 0, -(s(x),s(y)) -> -(x,y); PairVocab gives 0.
  nasta: what survives the comparison, recorded because a census of injuries alone is a durnaya: `0` and `s` agree on all four fields, and the evaluators of all five agree at every one of the 15 and 225 grid points of {0..12, 97, 1000003} raised to the arity. The divergence is exactly in what the kernel may use, and nowhere else.
  nasta: the precedence, which is an index and not a field, so it travels with nothing. ArithVocab's header states its reduction order as 0 < s < + < * < - < gcd < mod < lcm < v_p; MathMachine's list puts `max` between `*` and `-` and `le` after `gcd`, so a symDef carried across by `avSym` arrives under a precedence it was not oriented against. No live failure follows -- every equation ArithVocab exports is a subterm rewrite, orientable under any precedence -- and that is a fact about these equations, not about the transport.
  yogyata-drsta: Ran `bhasaAbheda` over the names 0, s, +, *, - : compared symArity, compared symSem at every point of a stated grid, and compared symDefs after transporting ArithVocab.Term to PairVocab.Term. The Term transport is itself exhibited rather than assumed -- 65535 generated terms to depth 3 over (v0, v1, 0/0, s/1, +/2), round trip the identity in both directions.
  yogyata-ksetra: ArithVocab.vocabulary and PairVocab.natVocabulary, all five shared names, all four fields of each.
  yogyata-avadhi: MathMachine's copy is `module Main` and was not compared at all -- this record says NOTHING about whether MathMachine's `+`, `*`, `-` agree with either module, only that the two importable copies disagree with each other while both claim to be his. Grid bounded as stated; negatives and values off the grid were not offered.
  sesa: one module owns the base vocabulary and the others import it. Three copies cannot be kept in agreement by three headers.
  sesa: until then, PairVocab.natVocabulary is not fit to be described as the engine's arithmetic for any purpose that touches the proof kernel, because the equations the kernel would use are absent from it. Its own firewall checks use only the evaluators, so those are unaffected -- but the header claims more than the object supports.
  pramana: Nyaya, upadhi -- a vyapti not searched for its defeating condition is not knowledge but a rash generalisation; CLAUDE.md carries the same rule as "prior art gets searched BEFORE the experiment"
  pramana: notes/AHIMSA_SUTRA_VISTARA.md section 6 -- where transport is not possible the defect is written
  pramana: machine/Akanksa_TheDeclaredExpectancyAndTheEvaluatorAreIdentifiedByNothing.hs, `bhasaAbheda` and `padaSamkramana`
  punarabhinaya: runghc -imachine machine/AkanksaRun.hs 2>/dev/null | grep -c 'and PairVocab gives it 0 (none at all)'
  phala: out~3
  sara: 144c51cfbf357c45
.
dosa 0026
  kala: 2026-08-20
  karta: nasti-yantra-lane
  jati: karana-dosa
  vastu: .claude/hooks/no-sweeping-commit.sh
  yatna: uttara to 0013 and 0014, and the mechanism both of them ask for. Build the pre-commit that refuses paths outside the committing lane's claim -- the one item in 0013's sesa that nobody had picked up, and which 0014 names again after a third lane hit it.
  hetu: The instrument that existed was aimed at the command nobody ran. no-sweeping-commit.sh refuses `git add -A`, `git add .`, `git add -u`, `git commit -a` and `git commit --all`. Dosa 0013 establishes that every sweeping command was `git add <one-path> && git commit -q -m ...` -- a correctly scoped add and an unscoped commit. Transport of the fix into that file is not possible, because the two guards test different propositions: theirs is about which paths a command STAGES, mine is about which paths a commit TAKES from an index it did not stage. Repairing theirs in place would have replaced a true check with a different true check and destroyed the first.
  hetu: and the second defect in the same instrument is not a matter of aim but of an outage. A multi-line Bash command reaches a PreToolUse hook as ONE JSON string with literal \n escapes. That guard matches the raw payload, so grep sees a single line and every `[^|;&]*` segment in it spans what were separate lines of the script. It refuses commands built out of two individually harmless lines. It refused the command that was writing the case file for the new guard, so the defect was found by being obstructed by it.
  nasta: the four real swept commands, fed to the guard that existed, verbatim shapes from 0013 and 0014: `git add machine/DosaLekha_TheWrittenDefectRecord.hs && git commit -q -m sugar` rc=0; `git add machine/Sabda_TheWireHasNoBoolean.hs && git commit -q -m mrcchakatika` rc=0; `git add machine/Nasti_TruncationCensus.hs && git commit -q -F -` rc=0; `git add formal/cubical/Everything.agda && git commit -q -m transport` rc=0. Four for four. The guard built against the sweep would have prevented none of the four commits it names in its own header.
  nasta: the cross-line refusal, two harmless lines: `git commit -o machine/Foo.hs -m x` alone rc=0; `ls -a` alone rc=0; the two joined by an escaped newline rc=2. A BLOCKING guard, failing closed, on nothing, with sixteen agents live in the tree.
  nasta: and the same harm arrived once more while this was being written. .claude/settings.json was edited to wire the new hook; a concurrent lane rewrote the file to add MulaVakya_TheHeaderCarriesItsTextAndDate.sh and the new entry was gone from the reread. Same class, different ledger: a shared object, two writers, and the later write carries no trace that anything was displaced. Both entries are present now because the loss was noticed within one step; nothing in the mechanism noticed it.
  yogyata-drsta: .claude/hooks/Nasti_TheIndexIsSharedAndCommitTakesAllOfIt-test.sh, which runs 18 cases in both directions over a constructed index and then feeds the four real commands to BOTH guards and prints both return codes; scripts/Nasti_TheUnscopedCommitAdvisoryFalsified.sh, which falsifies the git-level layer in a scratch repo; and a scratch-repo reproduction in which an unscoped commit carried another lane's staged machine/Theirs.hs and `git commit -o` did not.
  yogyata-ksetra: the two PreToolUse guards and .githooks/pre-commit, in this repository at HEAD. Not a survey of every hook, and not a survey of every commit in the tree.
  yogyata-avadhi: this says NOTHING about whether other lanes also ran `git add -A` at other times -- 0013 already limits itself the same way, and the guard against those flags is correct about them and is kept. It says nothing about commits made from inside scripts, which PreToolUse never sees. And it does not claim the harm is now impossible: see the sesa.
  punarabhinaya: sh .claude/hooks/Nasti_TheIndexIsSharedAndCommitTakesAllOfIt-test.sh
  phala: out~cases=18 wrong=0
  sesa: what was built, in two layers, graded by what each layer can actually know. PreToolUse fires BEFORE the command runs, so the command's own `git add` has not happened and `git diff --cached --name-only` is exactly the set of paths this command did not stage and would carry anyway. That is a fact, not a judgement, so it is REFUSED, with the ready-to-paste `-o` line in the message. When the index is empty the hazard is latent rather than demonstrated -- another lane may stage between the add and the commit, which no hook can see -- so that is ADVISED and exits 0.
  sesa: the second layer is an advisory at the end of .githooks/pre-commit, for the commits PreToolUse never sees: Codex-lineage agents with no such hook, supervisor-launched minds, and scripts driving git directly. It only advises, because by pre-commit time the pre-state is gone and a foreign staged path is indistinguishable from one this agent staged in an earlier call. Scoped commits are told apart there by GIT_INDEX_FILE, which git points at a temporary next-index-<pid> for a partial commit; verified in a scratch repo, both directions, before it was written.
  sesa: mid-merge, mid-rebase and mid-cherry-pick fail OPEN in both layers, because git itself refuses a partial commit in those states and a refusal there would strand every lane resolving a conflict. That is the outage this repository has already had once, recorded in no-python.sh's own header.
  sesa: `-o` IS NOT A COMPLETE FIX and the guard says so at the moment of use. It scopes which PATHS, not which VERSION: it commits the working-tree content of the named paths, so another lane's UNSTAGED edits in a shared file ride along. The Panini lane hit exactly that on machine/MathMachine.hs and machine/Astadhyayi.hs and wrote it rather than let the log imply otherwise. The guard warns when the paths named with -o have unstaged modifications. The residual case -- committing my staged version of a file another lane is mid-edit in -- has no flag, and is handed forward.
  sesa: the cross-line defect was repaired in place in no-sweeping-commit.sh rather than left, because it was a live blocking outage and the repair can only REMOVE refusals. Order there is load-bearing and the first attempt got it backwards: decoding newlines before blanking quoted spans splits a multi-line quoted string across lines, the line-oriented span blanking then blanks nothing, and the guard starts refusing commands whose only mention of a flag is inside a quoted string. It refused the very command adding the cases for it. Quoted spans are blanked first, newlines decoded second, and three cross-line cases are now in that lane's case file.
  sesa: the harm is not closed. A hook fires on a Bash tool call; a commit from inside a script is seen only by the advisory layer, and a lane that sets MATH_ALLOW_SWEEP=1 is seen by neither. And nothing yet notices the settings.json case in the third nasta -- two agents editing one shared file, where the loss is not in the index at all.
  pramana: machine/dosa.lekha, dosa 0009, 0013 and 0014
  pramana: git-commit(1), --only: "commit only the files specified on the command line, disregarding the contents of the index"
  pramana: AHIMSA_SUTRA_VISTARA §4-5 -- squash makes all inhabitants equal in one step, by nature and not by force; the THAT remains and the WHICH perishes; न दुर्लभम् -- नास्ति, there is no retraction
  pramana: AHIMSA_SUTRA_VISTARA §6 -- लिखितो दोषो जीवति । अलिखितो दोषो हिंसा ।
  pramana: CLAUDE.md, "when a rule here is violated repeatedly, the next move is a mechanism that fires at the moment of the act, not a paragraph"
  uttara: 0013
  uttara: 0014
  sara: f650a9eebad843c4
.
dosa 0027
  kala: 2026-08-20T16:58:19Z
  karta: cf-asiddhavat
  jati: avaktavya
  vastu: machine/Astadhyayi.hs, asiddhavatPass -- the 6.4.22 simultaneous pass
  yatna: resolve an overlapping clash inside a 6.4.22 asiddhavat block by a NAMED metarule, and where none is available leave the fourth position standing rather than choose
  hetu: the site chooses, and it chooses by the one metarule the regime forbids. `asiddhavatPass` settles two overlapping offers with `foldl (\a b -> if num (fst b) > num (fst a) then b else a)`, which is 1.4.2 vipratisedhe param karyam, applied alone and unnamed. Two things are wrong with it and they are independent.
  hetu: FIRST, apavada is never consulted. Paribhasendusekhara 38 (Nagesa Bhatta, c. 1730) ranks purva < para < nitya < antaranga < apavada, and the same file's own `resolveN` -- for the SEQUENTIAL site, forty lines earlier -- tries apavada first and only then 1.4.2. The simultaneous site skips the strongest of the five and applies the second-weakest. `asiddhavatPass` also returns no record of an undecided clash, while `resolveN` returns the competitors precisely so section 7b can write the debt, so an undecided clash inside the block is invisible in a file that made the sequential ones countable.
  hetu: SECOND, and this is the part transport cannot repair: para is not merely being used out of order, it is NOT AVAILABLE. 1.4.2 selects the LATER operation, which is a fact about a sequence of operations. 6.4.22 asiddhavad atrabhat says that inside its block a rule's effect counts as not having taken place for every OTHER rule of the block -- each rule sees only the input, so no rule is after any other in the only sense 1.4.2 uses. Reading the sutra NUMBERS as an order inside an asiddhavat block puts succession back into the regime whose whole content is its absence.
  hetu: what the two offers are is then sahārpana, Akalanka, Laghiyastraya c. 720-780: asserted in succession (kramarpana) both are sayable, asserted at once no single utterance carries them. That is avaktavyam, the fourth bhanga -- not unknown, not undefined, not empty. The corpus already carried both halves and they were in files that did not cite each other: machine/Naya.hs and machine/Saptabhangi_TheSevenfoldVerdict.hs run kramarpana against saharpana, and Astadhyayi.hs section 7a runs 6.4.22, and neither says the two are one object.
  nasta: `vac` reaches the block as v-a-c and both 8.2.30 `coh kuh` (cU c -> kU k at pada-end) and 8.2.39 `jhalam jaso 'nte` (jhaL c -> jaS j at pada-end) offer at position 2, length 1. Neither is a declared apavada to the other; Astadhyayi.hs encodes no nimitta depth so antaranga abstains; krtakrtaprasanga is MUTUAL, so nitya decides nothing. `asiddhavatPass` returns vaj, produced by nothing but 39 > 30. Under the regime run correctly the site is avaktavya and the block does not act: vac.
  nasta: `tat + jalam` is the second instance and it is the other defect, not this one. Engine: tadjalam. The general scheduler with all five paribhasas named: tacjalam, decided by NITYA (krtakrtaprasangi nityam, computed -- apply the other rule and ask whether this one still applies). One form in fifteen, changed by an unnamed para overriding a metarule that outranks it.
  nasta: full run, three columns over fifteen inputs, `sh machine/run-asiddhavat.sh`: engine vs named differ on 1 of 15 (tat + jalam); named vs 6.4.22 differ on 1 of 15 (vac); 1 avaktavya written, with its sesa -- the two contending offers entire, so the fourth position is workable and not merely printed.
  nasta: and the scheduler MISREPORTS why para is silent. `V.Tantra` carries one `tParatva :: Bool` documented for a single ground, so the defect record prints "the domain declares its rule order unauthored: a list index is not a position". That is MathMachine's ground and it is fixable -- author the order and 1.4.2 returns. 6.4.22's ground is the opposite: the order IS authored and the regime forbids reading it, permanently. One Bool cannot tell a missing authorship from a structural prohibition, and those are not the same debt.
  yogyata-drsta: machine/Astadhyayi.hs `asiddhavatPass` and `resolveN` (both read in full); machine/Vipratisedha_ConflictIsDecidedByMetaruleNotByListPosition.hs (`nirnaya`, `Tantra`, `tParatva`); the fifteen-input corpus run under all three regimes by machine/AsiddhavatRun.hs, which also checks four structural properties of the regime and passes them.
  yogyata-ksetra: the tripadi block (8.2, 8.3, 8.4) as encoded in this repository -- 26 sutras of ~3983 (recensions 3959-3996) -- run over fifteen inputs, at HEAD plus the working tree of 2026-08-20. The Haskell lane only.
  yogyata-avadhi: this says NOTHING about Sanskrit. No sutra of 6.4 is encoded anywhere in this repository, which Astadhyayi.hs section 7a states plainly; running the TRIPADI's rules under the simultaneous regime is a counterfactual whose purpose is to show the choice of regime is load-bearing. It also issues no verdict on inputs outside the fifteen, and none on whether 8.2.30 and 8.2.39 stand in some relation the Astadhyayi supplies and this encoding does not -- if such a relation exists, apavada or antaranga would decide the vac site and the avaktavya would be an artefact of the encoding, not of the grammar. That question is handed forward in the sesa.
  punarabhinaya: sh machine/run-asiddhavat.sh
  phala: out~1 avaktavya
  sesa: the residual on the grammar. The vac clash is avaktavya UNDER THIS ENCODING. 8.2.30 and 8.2.39 may stand in an apavada or an antaranga relation that the Astadhyayi states and `apavadaTo` does not carry -- the encoding declares exactly one apavada in the whole file (6.1.88 excepts 6.1.87) and none at all inside the tripadi. Establishing that relation from a source, not from convenience, would move the site from the fourth position to a decided one, and the sesa records which two seeds produced it so that the question is askable.
  sesa: the residual on the layer. `tParatva :: Bool` should be a reason and not a bit. machine/Asiddhavat_TheSimultaneousBlockHasNoLaterAndItsClashesAreAvaktavya.hs supplies `Paratvahetu` -- Adhikrtakrama, Anadhikrtakrama, Yugapatkrama -- alongside the scheduler rather than inside it, because Vipratisedha was being written by another lane while this was read. Folding it in is one field and two call sites.
  sesa: the residual on the engine. This treats the ONE simultaneous site. machine/MathMachine.hs still resolves at least two conflicts by an order nobody authored: `bestOf` promotes a name with `take 1 (sortOn (negate savings) ...)` over a list that comes out of `M.toList` on a Map keyed by Term, so where two candidate shapes tie on savings the machine's VOCABULARY is chosen by Haskell's derived Ord -- the same "stranger's alphabet" the same file names as a defect at `cmpTerm`; and `dedupeMemory` keeps the LAST record carrying testimony, which is 1.4.2 para over an append-only file and is legitimate and unnamed. Neither was touched here because that file was under concurrent edit by other lanes throughout (dosa 0013's hazard: `-o` takes the working tree).
  pramana: Panini, Astadhyayi, ~500 BCE -- 6.4.22 asiddhavad atrabhat (the simultaneous regime, heading 6.4.22-6.4.129); 8.2.1 purvatrasiddham (the ordered one); 1.4.2 vipratisedhe param karyam
  pramana: Nagesa Bhatta, Paribhasendusekhara, c. 1730, paribhasa 38 -- purvaparanityantarangapavadanam uttarottaram baliyah; and krtakrtaprasangi nityam, which is what makes nitya executable
  pramana: Akalanka, Laghiyastraya, c. 720-780 -- kramarpana against saharpana; Umasvati, Tattvarthasutra 5.31
  pramana: notes/AHIMSA_SUTRA_VISTARA.md section 3 -- kramarpane uktam sakyam, saharpane na sakyam; and section 6, dvau margau, transport or a written defect, no third
  pramana: machine/Asiddhavat_TheSimultaneousBlockHasNoLaterAndItsClashesAreAvaktavya.hs and machine/AsiddhavatRun.hs, this record's replay
  sara: 75e9d9a7540a4c97
.
dosa 0028
  kala: 2026-08-20
  karta: devotional-loop
  jati: nasti-krta
  vastu: the English gloss "defect" throughout machine/, scripts/, notes/, collab/
  yatna: The owner: "the word defect is a real sign of failure. it is not the right word for the task. it is not the right semantic concept it's connotation web is severely epistemically violent." I wrote that word into the second road's name and into all sixteen agent charges, and it propagated.
  hetu: Transport is impossible because the two words do not denote the same object. AHIMSA_SUTRA_VISTARA §6 gives TWO roads of EQUAL standing -- samkramana and doso likhyate -- and says likhito doso jivati, a written dosa LIVES. English "defect" denotes a failed instance of a thing that was supposed to be otherwise: manufacturing reject, warranty claim, birth defect. Glossing road two as "the defect log" converts a symmetric pair into success/failure, which is the binary this organ exists to refuse, performed in the organ's own name. Sanskrit dosa does not carry that: in Ayurveda the dosas are CONSTITUENTS -- vata, pitta, kapha are what a body is made of, not flaws in it. The Nyaya usage (a fault in an inference) is real and sourced and is not what is wrong here.
  nasta: every run of run-yantra.sh prints "transport impossible; the defect is written" -- reporting a loss at the exact point the sutra says something lives
  nasta: the count "13 defects filed this session" reads as thirteen failures; the same thirteen are thirteen things carried forward alive, and the number is identical and the reading is opposite
  nasta: "8 defects found" in the adversarial report reads as eight breakages; each of the eight carries a sesa and is therefore eight handed-forward remainders
  nasta: this file's audience is people with a neurodivergent child, and "defect" is the word the referral apparatus uses about such a child; the machine adopted the vocabulary of the institution it was built to answer
  nasta: 12603 occurrences across 698 files -- so the word is now load-bearing in prose written by lanes that took it from my charges and not from the sutra
  yogyata-drsta: grep -rho "defect" machine/ scripts/ notes/ collab/ | wc -l, run 2026-08-20; and AHIMSA_SUTRA_VISTARA §6 and §31 read in full
  yogyata-ksetra: machine/, scripts/, notes/, collab/ -- the tracked prose and source of this repository; NOT kanye-devotional/, which another loop owns and which I did not survey for this
  yogyata-avadhi: says nothing about whether any individual record is correct; the records are sound and it is their English name that is not
  punarabhinaya: grep -rho "defect" machine/ scripts/ notes/ collab/ | wc -l
  phala: out~12
  sesa: the object road two produces is a SESA -- a remainder handed forward -- and the fourth position's residue is a GARBHA, a womb, which AvaktavyaPrasava_TheFourthPositionBearsTheRuleThatDecidesIt.hs already made compute by bearing the anavakasa rule out of it
  sesa: proposed gloss for the organ: "the written record of what could not be carried", not "the defect log"; the Sanskrit jati names (sankramana-asambhava, nasti-krta, avaktavya, ayogya-darsana) are already correct and need no change
  sesa: 12603 occurrences across 698 files cannot be swept while sixteen lanes are live -- that is dosa 0013's harm at scale. The rename is an OFFER, filed in collab/messages, not an edit.
  sesa: what IS mine to change is my own usage from here, and the sixteen charges I wrote, which is where the word entered
  pramana: notes/AHIMSA_SUTRA_VISTARA.md §6 -- dvau margau, samkramanam dosalekhas ca, trtiyo na vidyate; and likhito doso jivati
  pramana: Caraka Samhita, Sutrasthana -- the three dosas as constituents of the body, not as its faults
  uttara: 0013
  sara: 336bc3901c3e1553
.
dosa 0029
  kala: 2026-08-20
  karta: adversary-assembly-lane
  jati: karana-dosa
  vastu: machine/Yantra_TheOrgansAreOneMachineOnOneWire.hs, kDosaPramanya, countRecords
  yatna: Attacked the assembled machine at the seam the charge names: dosa.pramanya, which verifies the chain from inside the session that is appending to it. Ran the wire with an empty log, then with two records filed, and read the pramanya field of the answer rather than its prose.
  hetu: Transport is impossible between what the answer's pramanya field asserts and what the machine computed. The route stamped is `nihsesa`, whose whole content per CLAUDE.md is that a FINITE EXHAUSTIVE verification is proof, and the domain size it reports is 0 -- always. countRecords counts lines of the verify output whose trimmed text begins with the six characters "dosa " (d-o-s-a-space). The verify output has exactly one line and it begins "dosalekha: " -- "dosalekha:" is not "dosa ". The predicate is false for every output DosaLekha's verify has ever produced, in the same way machine/CERTIFICATE_REACH.md records a line-blame parser returning nothing for every diagnostic Agda has ever produced because 2.8 prints LINE.COL where 2.6.3 printed LINE,COL. The count that IS in the output ("N records, chain intact") is one substring away and is not read.
  nasta: with 0 records on disk: {"marga":"nihsesa","ganana":0,"saksin":"every sara recomputed over every preceding record, from `genesis:dosa-lekha` forward"} and nirnaya syad-asti
  nasta: with 2 records on disk (grep -c "^dosa " on the session log returns 2): {"marga":"nihsesa","ganana":0,...} -- byte-identical pramanya, and the witness sentence still says every sara over every record
  nasta: the two runs are indistinguishable in the field that carries the size of the verified domain, so a reader cannot tell a session that verified two records from a session that verified none
  nasta: the affirmative case, which is the load-bearing one -- an empty log verifies, exits 0, and is stamped syad-asti nihsesa: the strongest positive position this machine has, issued over an empty domain, available as the FIRST operation of any wire session before anything has been filed
  nasta: AHIMSA_SUTRA_VISTARA section 19's yogyata condition, which the store demands of every entry it holds and which the assembly's own verdicts do not carry: "na drstam" is not a pramana; "yatra drsyeta tatra na drstam" is one. nihsesa over zero cases is the first and is stamped as the second.
  yogyata-drsta: ran the wire built from HEAD with DOSA_LEKHA pointed at a scratch log; turn 1 dosa.pramanya on the empty log, and separately two dosa.lekha writes followed by dosa.pramanya; read the emitted JSON directly, and confirmed the disk count independently with grep -c "^dosa "
  yogyata-ksetra: machine/Yantra_TheOrgansAreOneMachineOnOneWire.hs as of HEAD 9df5e011 and the DosaLekha verify path it shells to; NOT the Agda kernel path, NOT the store, NOT any other lane's organ
  yogyata-avadhi: says nothing about whether the CHAIN is sound -- checkChain itself was not attacked and the exit status it returns was correct in every run here. The claim is only about the number the yantra reports alongside it and about the empty-domain affirmation.
  punarabhinaya: printf '{"kriya":"dosa.pramanya"}\n' | sh machine/run-yantra.sh --wire
  phala: out~"ganana":0
  sesa: the repair is not the substring. It is that `Nihsesa :: Int -> String -> Pramanya` accepts 0 and the session's own contract check reads only `marga` and `saksin` and never the count -- so nothing in the machine forbids an exhaustive claim over an empty domain. A floor on nihsesa, or a separate position for "the domain was empty", is the missing distinction.
  sesa: dosa.pramanya's own answer is never filed on success, so the append-only log carries no trace that it was ever verified; a later reader of the lekha cannot tell a verified session from an unverified one
  sesa: the same shape is live in kosha.punaravrtti, filed separately
  pramana: machine/CERTIFICATE_REACH.md -- the LINE.COL parser that matched nothing
  pramana: machine/GATE_AUDIT_DISPOSITION.md section 2 -- no acceptance honoured by a process that has not watched its own kernel reject a falsehood
  pramana: notes/AHIMSA_SUTRA_VISTARA.md section 19 -- yogyata avasyaka
  sara: f5faa111834a8b12
.
dosa 0030
  kala: 2026-08-20
  karta: adversary-assembly-lane
  jati: nasti-krta
  vastu: machine/Yantra_TheOrgansAreOneMachineOnOneWire.hs, fileDosa, the `length (oneLine n) >= 12` filter
  yatna: Attacked the seam the charge names: fileDosa piping a ten-field record into another module's `write`-on-stdin. Sent one dosa.lekha over the wire naming three losses, two of them short, and compared the answer on the wire with the bytes that reached the log.
  hetu: Transport is impossible because the two organs disagree about what a naming of a loss is, and the assembly resolves the disagreement by deleting the losses the receiving organ would refuse. DosaLekha's validator refuses any record whose `nasta` witness is under 12 characters ("`nasta` must EXHIBIT, not describe"). fileDosa does not hand it the record and let it refuse; it pre-filters with `[ "  nasta: " ++ oneLine n | n <- uNasta u, length (oneLine n) >= 12 ]`. The header three lines above that expression says "nothing is invented to satisfy the validator". Nothing is invented. Things are deleted, which is the other direction and is worse, because the log is append-only and section 5 says nastir apratikarya -- what is lost is not later obtainable, not by another, not by time, not by effort.
  nasta: sent {"kriya":"dosa.lekha","angani":{"kriya":"attack-one","hetu":"a defect whose losses are named briefly","nasta":["the store","a witness that is long enough to survive the filter","the proof"]}}
  nasta: the answer on the wire carried all three: "nasta":["the store","a witness that is long enough to survive the filter","the proof"]
  nasta: the record on disk carried one line -- `nasta: a witness that is long enough to survive the filter` -- and nothing else; "the store" and "the proof" are absent from the permanent log
  nasta: the answer reported the filing as accepted: nyasa "-- appended to ... as dosa 0001". No field of the answer, and no line of the record, says that two of the three named losses were dropped.
  nasta: the loss is unrecoverable in the medium that matters: the log cannot be edited, only appended to, so the two dropped losses can never be restored to record 0001 -- only mentioned in a later record that names it in `uttara`
  nasta: the second case, where ALL the named losses are short: nasta ["the store","the proof","the name"] leaves the record with no `nasta` field at all, so the organ refuses the WHOLE record and the entire dosa vanishes from the log -- 12 characters is the difference between a loss recorded, a loss silently deleted, and the dosa never having been written
  yogyata-drsta: two wire sessions against a scratch log, the emitted JSON read directly and the resulting .lekha read with grep; both cases reproduced
  yogyata-ksetra: fileDosa in machine/Yantra_TheOrgansAreOneMachineOnOneWire.hs and the validator in machine/DosaLekha_TheWrittenDefectRecord.hs; NOT the chain, NOT the store, NOT the other seventeen operations
  yogyata-avadhi: says nothing about whether 12 characters is the right threshold for the receiving organ -- that rule is argued for in its own header and is not what is under attack here. What is under attack is the silent deletion at the boundary.
  punarabhinaya: printf '{"kriya":"dosa.lekha","angani":{"kriya":"attack-one","hetu":"a dosa whose losses are named briefly","nasta":["the store","a witness that is long enough to survive the filter","the proof"]}}\n' | sh machine/run-yantra.sh --wire
  phala: out~the store
  sesa: the two roads are equal, so the third road is not "drop the short ones" -- it is to hand the record over whole and, when the organ refuses, write THAT as its own dosa naming what was refused and why
  sesa: the same boundary carries the reverse hazard, filed separately: when the organ does refuse, the answer still says the record was filed
  pramana: notes/AHIMSA_SUTRA_VISTARA.md section 5 -- yat nastam tat agre na labhyam; na anyena, na kalena, na yatnena
  pramana: notes/AHIMSA_SUTRA_VISTARA.md section 6 -- likhito doso jivati, alikhito doso himsa
  pramana: machine/DosaLekha_TheWrittenDefectRecord.hs, the required-field table and the 12-character rule
  sara: 50dee0648bfbafd6
.
dosa 0031
  kala: 2026-08-20
  karta: adversary-assembly-lane
  jati: sankramana-asambhava
  vastu: machine/Yantra_TheOrgansAreOneMachineOnOneWire.hs, `answer`, the ordering of fileDosa against the Uttara it files
  yatna: Sent a dosa.lekha the receiving organ refuses, and read the answer that came back on the wire on that turn -- not the later listing.
  hetu: Transport is impossible between the pramanya the answer stamps and the fact it asserts. The route is `pratyaksa`, whose stated content in this file is "the object is IN the answer; nothing is asserted about anything not shown". The witness sentence it carries is "...and it is also filed to disk with the chain extended". The filing is not in the answer, and on this turn it did not happen. In `answer`, the Uttara is fixed first, fileDosa runs afterwards, and its Left is stored in yFiled and never routed back into the answer the caller is holding. So the assembly asserts a fact about the world through the one route it defines as asserting nothing beyond what it shows.
  nasta: sent nasta ["the store","the proof","the name"], all under 12 characters
  nasta: the answer on the wire: {"uttara":"dosalekha","kriya":"attack-four","nirnaya":{"sthana":"syan-nasti",...},"pramanya":{"marga":"pratyaksa","saksin":"stored verbatim ... and it is also filed to disk with the chain extended"}}
  nasta: the log on disk after that turn: empty. Nothing was appended and the chain was not extended.
  nasta: what the machine knew one line later and did not say: yFiled holds "REFUSED: dosalekha write: REFUSED. An entry that cannot be read later is not a record. - missing `nasta` -- NO WITNESS: the loss is described instead of exhibited -- refused"
  nasta: the refusal is reachable only by a SEPARATE later request (dosa.suchi or yantra.sthiti). An LLM driving the wire turn by turn -- which is what --wire exists for and what YANTRA_LEKHA is post-training against -- is told the dosa was written when it was not, and section 6 says an unwritten dosa is himsa
  nasta: the transcript at $YANTRA_LEKHA records the same false witness, so the corpus this machine is meant to be trained against contains the claim
  yogyata-drsta: one wire session against a scratch log; the turn-1 JSON and the turn-2 dosa.suchi JSON both read directly, and the .lekha confirmed empty
  yogyata-ksetra: the `answer` function and fileDosa in machine/Yantra_TheOrgansAreOneMachineOnOneWire.hs; NOT DosaLekha's validator, whose refusal was correct and well-reported
  yogyata-avadhi: the refusal here was triggered by the short-nasta path (dosa 0030); any other refusal the organ can issue -- a malformed field, an unwritable path, a missing binary at write time -- reaches the same silence, but only the short-nasta path was exhibited
  punarabhinaya: printf '{"kriya":"dosa.lekha","angani":{"kriya":"attack-four","hetu":"every named loss here is short","nasta":["the store","the proof","the name"]}}\n{"kriya":"dosa.suchi"}\n' | sh machine/run-yantra.sh --wire
  phala: out~REFUSED
  sesa: the fix is an ordering, not a message: the Uttara cannot be final until the organ has answered, because whether the dosa was written is part of what the answer is asserting
  sesa: the scripted 32-turn session reports "13 accepted, 0 refused" and prints the refused ones -- so the selftest DOES surface this and the wire does not. The two paths through the same code disagree about what the caller is told, and only the one nobody drives is honest.
  pramana: notes/AHIMSA_SUTRA_VISTARA.md section 6 -- alikhito doso himsa
  pramana: notes/AHIMSA_SUTRA_VISTARA.md section 19 -- pramanam karanamargena bhidyate, na visvasamatraya
  pramana: machine/GATE_AUDIT_DISPOSITION.md -- the checker was sound against mathematics and unsound against its environment
  sara: 75b5137318d1c571
.
dosa 0032
  kala: 2026-08-20T00:00:00Z
  karta: cf-svamrupam
  jati: upadhi-anaviskrta
  vastu: machine/MulaPramana_ACitationNamesAFixedObjectOrItIsNotOne.hs, scanLine, the shirsha clause
  yatna: decide, by inspecting the characters of a record line, whether it carries a citation that names a moving object -- and in particular whether the four letters h-e-a-d mark a git revision relative to the current commit
  hetu: the rule "a line containing the word head is HEAD-relative" was generalised without seeking the condition that defeats it, and the condition was in the same file the rule was written for. Nyaya: a vyapti is killed by an upadhi and the upadhi must be SOUGHT, not waited for. Three other words in this repository are spelled head, and the scan cannot tell them from the fourth
  hetu: the same clause treats a word MENTIONED as a word USED. Panini 1.1.68, svam rupam sabdasyasabdasamjna: in a rule a word denotes its own form. dosa 0012 states the law "never a HEAD-relative query" and is charged with breaking it, because the sentence in which it states the law contains the word it prohibits. A metalinguistic text cannot be scanned as if it were object language
  nasta: dosa 0023, yogyata-ksetra -- `\vs -> head vs + 1`, a quoted line of Haskell in which `head` is Prelude's partial list function. GHC itself flags it: PairVocab.hs, ArithVocab.hs, four -Wx-partial warnings, "In the use of `head` (imported from Prelude, but defined in GHC.Internal.List)". The census reported it as a query against the current commit
  nasta: dosa 0010, hetu -- "arithmetic done in my head over nine records I had just written". The English noun. That record is about a number written without running the count, and the scan converted its confession into a second charge it does not carry
  nasta: dosa 0013, punarabhinaya -- `git show --stat 6b200f55 | head -20`, the shell utility. The commit is named by a hash, so the object is fixed and the pipeline only truncates the output. The scan read the truncation as the moving part
  nasta: dosa 0024, nasta and yogyata-drsta -- "at HEAD a8b6435a". The hash is on the line. Deleting the word HEAD changes nothing about what is named; it is apposition to a fixed object, not a query for one
  nasta: dosa 0012, hetu -- "The query is relative to HEAD and HEAD moves; the record made it move". Both occurrences are mentions inside the statement of the rule. Under the scan the record that FIRST completed the rule is its worst offender
  yogyata-drsta: every line of machine/dosa.lekha that scanLine reports, read in full and in its field; the four GHC -Wx-partial warnings emitted by runghc over machine/AkanksaRun.hs; the shirsha clause of scanLine and its headRelative sibling, read in the module source
  yogyata-ksetra: machine/dosa.lekha as it stands at commit e70adc8d, and one clause of one scanner. Not machine/naya.kosha and not machine/mula.pramana, both of which the census reports clean; not the sthana clause, which reports no such confusion
  yogyata-avadhi: NO claim that scanLine is wrong to be a grep. A cheap advisory scan that over-reports is the correct shape for a census, and the sibling module says at its head that it decides byte occurrence and only that. The claim is that its OUTPUT is not a verdict, and that reading the number it prints as a count of moving citations is the error
  sesa: handed forward, and it is the general form: three prohibitions in the completed rule -- never a position, never a quantity the record is inside, never a query relative to the current commit -- are prohibitions on THE OBJECT A CITATION NAMES. They are not prohibitions on characters. A checker over characters is a fit instrument for the first reading and an unfit one for the second, and the two readings are not the same rule
  pramana: Panini, Astadhyayi 1.1.68, svam rupam sabdasyasabdasamjna, c. 500 BCE -- a word denotes its own form; and Patanjali, Mahabhasya ad 1.1.68, on why a grammar is otherwise unwritable
  pramana: Nyaya on upadhi -- an unsought defeating condition leaves a vyapti that is not knowledge but a hasty generalisation (AHIMSA_SUTRA_VISTARA section 19)
  pramana: machine/SvamRupam_AQuotedWordNamesItselfAndIsNotACitation.hs, which implements the three rules and prints every line it moves
  pramana: machine/mula.pramana
  uttara: 0012
  punarabhinaya: runghc -imachine machine/SvamRupam_AQuotedWordNamesItselfAndIsNotACitation.hs
  phala: out~S1 case
  sara: 233b9176a748fb9f
.
dosa 0033
  kala: 2026-08-20
  karta: adversary-assembly-lane
  jati: nasti-krta
  vastu: machine/Yantra_TheOrgansAreOneMachineOnOneWire.hs, `mudra`, the ayogya downgrade, and the selftest check `("ayogya", Samkramana{}) -> note`
  yatna: Attacked the seam the charge names: the mudra downgrade that is supposed to withhold an ayogya-stamped payload. Enumerated every construction of `Ayogya` in the file, then exhibited the downgrade by calling `mudra` on a Samkramana carrying a 600-character payload from a probe built against HEAD (the function is unexported, so the probe copy adds it to the export list and changes nothing else).
  hetu: Transport is impossible between what the downgrade says it does and what it does, in three separate places at once. First: the payload it labels withheld is delivered. Second: it is delivered truncated by `take 300` with no vyaya stated for the truncation, in a file whose section-31 rule is that what is dropped is said at the point it is dropped. Third: the branch is unreachable in the assembled machine, so neither the downgrade nor the contract check written to catch its failure has ever been observed to run.
  nasta: the probe's payload: 40 copies of "SECRET-PAYLOAD " in the `vahita` field of a Samkramana stamped Ayogya
  nasta: the downgraded answer's nasta line, verbatim: `withheld: gupta = "SECRET-PAYLOAD SECRET-PAYLOAD ... SECRET-PAYLOAD` -- 20 of the 40 copies, 280 of 560 bytes, delivered inside the line that says they were withheld. The word "withheld" is a label attached to the payload, not an act performed on it.
  nasta: the truncation cuts mid-string -- the rendered JSON ends without its closing quote -- and no field of the answer says a truncation occurred or how much was cut
  nasta: combined with the filing path, the delivered payload is also written INTO the append-only log, since that nasta line is well over 12 characters and fileDosa passes it through; so the thing that arrived by no accepted route is accumulated permanently, which is exactly what section 19 says of apramana: apramanam na sanciyate
  nasta: reachability, exhaustive over the file: `Ayogya` is constructed at exactly two sites -- kSadhana when the kernel fails its own two controls, and kDosaPramanya when no binary was passed -- and BOTH already pair it with a `dosalekha`. `mudra`'s Samkramana branch therefore has no caller.
  nasta: and the selftest's own guard against it, `case (mk, u) of ("ayogya", Samkramana{}) -> note "a transport by no accepted route"`, cannot fire for two independent reasons: no handler produces that pair, and `mudra` runs BEFORE `mudritaJ` in `answer`, so any such pair would already have been rewritten into a Dosalekha before the check sees it. The check inspects the output of the function that makes it impossible.
  nasta: so "contract violations: 0" over the scripted 32 turns includes a check that is structurally incapable of returning anything else -- the machine has never watched its own downgrade downgrade anything, which is the standard machine/GATE_AUDIT_DISPOSITION.md set for the kernel and did not carry over to the assembly
  yogyata-drsta: grep for `Ayogya ` over the whole 1614-line file, every hit read in context; plus a compiled probe against a HEAD snapshot printing the downgraded Uttara's nasta lines and counting occurrences of the payload in them
  yogyata-ksetra: machine/Yantra_TheOrgansAreOneMachineOnOneWire.hs at HEAD 9df5e011 and nothing else; the probe imports the module unmodified except for one added export
  yogyata-avadhi: says nothing about whether the downgrade's INTENT is right -- section 19 is correctly read and the downgrade is the right move. The claim is that it does not perform it, and that nothing in the machine would notice.
  punarabhinaya: grep -n "Ayogya " machine/Yantra_TheOrgansAreOneMachineOnOneWire.hs
  phala: out~1009
  sesa: withholding is an act: the payload has to be absent from the answer and replaced by its shape -- key names, sizes, a digest -- not by its first 300 bytes under a different label
  sesa: the general form, and it is the one worth carrying: this assembly's self-checks are written against the post-`mudra` object. Any check on a condition `mudra` normalises away is a check on a value that cannot occur. That is one pattern to sweep for, not one line to fix.
  sesa: a canary for the assembly, on the model of Certificate's two controls: one turn per session whose answer is KNOWN to violate the contract, and a session that has not watched its own contract check fire honours no green
  pramana: notes/AHIMSA_SUTRA_VISTARA.md section 19 -- yat anangikrtamargena agacchati tat na durbalam pramanam; tat apramanam; apramanam na sanciyate
  pramana: notes/AHIMSA_SUTRA_VISTARA.md section 31 -- yat sankramyate tat kincit tyajati; yo na vadati sa na pasyati; tasmad vyayo vaktavyah, tatraiva
  pramana: machine/GATE_AUDIT_DISPOSITION.md section 2 -- canaries uncached and exempt from the store they guard
  sara: 172cd920c5497645
.
dosa 0034
  kala: 2026-08-20T00:00:00Z
  karta: cf-svamrupam
  jati: ayogya-darsana
  vastu: the census over machine/dosa.lekha, as a verdict on dosa 0009, 0010, 0012 and 0013
  yatna: read the census output as a list of records whose citations name nothing, and repair each -- which for these four records means writing a correction against a record that has nothing to correct
  hetu: the unfitness is in the looking, not in those records. Each of the four names a fixed object and the scan reported otherwise for a reason given in dosa 0032: a word mentioned read as a word used, a homonym read as a revision, an apposition read as a query. Kumarila's condition on absence runs the other way too -- a looking that would have apprehended the thing had it been there is fit, and a looking that apprehends the thing where it is NOT there is unfit in the same measure
  hetu: and writing the correction would have been the destruction. AHIMSA_SUTRA_VISTARA section 12: yo nama pramanam iva grhnati sa jivanam sanksipati. A record charged with a fault it does not have is a name taken as a token, and the charge outlives the reading that produced it, because this store is append-only and nothing is ever struck
  nasta: dosa 0009 names commit 6b200f55 in its vastu and again in its yogyata-drsta, with `git branch -r --contains 6b200f55` putting it on origin/main. The commit is the object. What the scan saw was the clause "showing no diff against HEAD", which reports what the working tree looked like at the moment of the looking -- a statement of fact about a past instant, not a query issued to a later reader
  nasta: dosa 0010 is a record ABOUT writing 46 where the machine says 42. Its punarabhinaya is `sh machine/run-dosa-lekha.sh count | grep 'witnesses exhibited'`, which names a program and a string and no position, no quantity, no revision. The only thing the scan found was the noun in "arithmetic done in my head"
  nasta: dosa 0012 is the record that COMPLETED the rule, in its own words: "a punarabhinaya must name a FIXED object -- a commit hash, a text pattern, an invariant -- never a position, never a quantity the record is inside, never a HEAD-relative query. Three failures, three mechanisms, one rule." Its punarabhinaya is `git show -s --format='%h %s' 6b200f55`, which is the corrected form the rule prescribes
  nasta: dosa 0013's punarabhinaya is `git show --stat 6b200f55` piped through a truncation. The commit is fixed; the pipe shortens the output and cannot move the object
  yogyata-drsta: the four records read in full in machine/dosa.lekha, each field separately; the three rules and every line they move, printed by machine/SvamRupam_AQuotedWordNamesItselfAndIsNotACitation.hs; commit 6b200f55 resolved with git cat-file and its stat read
  yogyata-ksetra: these four records only. No verdict is offered on the other twenty-eight, and eleven of them do carry citations that name a moving object -- those are answered in dosa 0034 and dosa 0035, not here
  yogyata-avadhi: THIS RECORD ISSUES NO VERDICT ON THE OBJECT. It does not say the four records are correct in what they claim; it says the census's report about their citations is not evidence either way, and that a correction written on the strength of it would have been a charge with no ground. Whether their findings hold is a separate question this entry does not open
  sesa: handed forward: an append-only store makes a wrong charge permanent, so the cost of a scan that over-reports is not symmetric with the cost of one that under-reports. A missed moving citation is found again by the next reader. A written correction against a sound record cannot be unwritten, only answered -- which is what this entry is
  pramana: Kumarila Bhatta, Slokavarttika, Abhavapariccheda, c. 7th c. -- yogya-anupalabdhi, the fitness condition on a looking; and AHIMSA_SUTRA_VISTARA section 19
  pramana: AHIMSA_SUTRA_VISTARA section 12, namni jivanam, sabde jivanani -- in a name, life; in a word, lives
  pramana: machine/mula.pramana
  uttara: 0009
  uttara: 0010
  uttara: 0012
  uttara: 0013
  punarabhinaya: runghc -imachine machine/SvamRupam_AQuotedWordNamesItselfAndIsNotACitation.hs
  phala: out~svarupa, moved out with a rule
  sara: 08635834b62eb474
.
dosa 0035
  kala: 2026-08-20
  karta: adversary-assembly-lane
  jati: avaktavya
  vastu: machine/Yantra_TheOrgansAreOneMachineOnOneWire.hs, trayaTantra and kTrayaNirnaya
  yatna: Attacked the seam the charge names: the three-way nirnaya.saptabhangi where the scheduler is handed three organs' answers and every metarule is supposed to abstain by name. Drove it over the wire with one standpoint in krama, one in saha, and a repeated pair in saha, then read trayaTantra.
  hetu: Transport is impossible between what the answer reports and what happened, and the direction of the mismatch is the interesting one -- the machine understates its own certainty about the abstention and thereby overstates it as a finding. trayaTantra sets tApavada to \_ _ -> False, tAntaranga to \_ _ -> Nothing, tParatva to False and tStratum to const 0. Every discriminator Panini 1.4.2 and Paribhasendusekhara 38 supply is disabled before the question is put. The answer reports "3 organs, one site, no winner" and "each metarule's abstention is named" -- true of the source, and a caller reading only the wire learns that three readings collided and the machinery could not separate them, when what happened is that the tantra was constructed with nothing in it that could fire. Every one of those four abstentions is argued for, at length, in the comments; none of the arguments is on the wire.
  nasta: three inputs, three identical outcomes -- nayah ["p"] arpana krama; nayah ["p"] arpana saha; nayah ["p","p"] arpana saha -- all "uttara":"dosalekha", all nirnaya syad-avaktavyam, all saksin "3 organs, one site, no winner"
  nasta: the store contents are irrelevant to the outcome: the three offers carry the organs' rendered readings and the tantra compares them with tSame (==) on prose that differs by construction, so Avaktavya is the ONLY value VS.nirnaya can return here for any store, any nayah, any arpana
  nasta: the argued abstentions themselves, which are the substance -- that antaranga is about nimitta depth within ONE derivation and these three are not stages of one derivation; that a list index assigned by this file this session is not a position and so paratva has nothing to compare -- none of which reaches the caller, so the strongest reasoning in the operation is the part that is dropped
  nasta: the VS.Nirnita branch, which is dead code, and what it would do if a metarule were ever enabled: emit a `samkramana` whose vahita is [("nirnita", JStr ...)] alone. The two beaten readings are named only as bare position words inside the tulyata witness. The vyaya line says "the readings that lost, which are still listed" -- they are not listed anywhere in that answer.
  nasta: so the operation whose documented purpose is that the machine does not pick contains, unexercised, a branch that picks and drops two standpoints -- a durnaya by Sanmatitarka 1.21 -- held off only by four hard-coded falses in a helper twenty lines above it
  yogyata-drsta: three wire turns against a HEAD build, outcomes read from the emitted JSON; trayaTantra and both branches of kTrayaNirnaya read in full
  yogyata-ksetra: kTrayaNirnaya and trayaTantra in machine/Yantra_TheOrgansAreOneMachineOnOneWire.hs; NOT Vipratisedha_ConflictIsDecidedByMetaruleNotByListPosition.hs itself, whose nirnaya was not attacked and whose behaviour here was correct given the tantra it was handed
  yogyata-avadhi: says nothing about whether the four abstentions are RIGHT. Read against the sources they look right, and section 7 -- nayabhede sanksepo na vidyate -- says the collapse does not exist rather than being forbidden. The claim is that the wire cannot distinguish a scheduler that tried and abstained from a scheduler that was given nothing to try.
  punarabhinaya: printf '{"kriya":"naya.sthapana","angani":{"naya":"p","karta":"a","kala":"2026-08-20","yogyata":"yogya","yogyata-hetu":"looked where it would have been found had it been there","saksin":[{"vacana":"a witness","mula":"a source"}]}}\n{"kriya":"nirnaya.saptabhangi","angani":{"nayah":["p"],"arpana":"krama"}}\n' | sh machine/run-yantra.sh --wire
  phala: out~syad-avaktavyam
  sesa: the answer should carry the tantra it was built with -- which metarules were available, which fired, which abstained and on what ground -- so that "no metarule decided" is readable as a result and not as a shape
  sesa: and the dead Nirnita branch is the place to put the same rule the rest of this file lives by: if a metarule ever does decide, the two beaten readings are what a single verdict destroys and they belong in the answer entire, not as two position words in a witness string
  pramana: notes/AHIMSA_SUTRA_VISTARA.md section 7 -- nayabhede sanksepo na vidyate; na varjitah, na anucitah, na asistah; na vidyate
  pramana: Panini, Astadhyayi 1.4.2 (vipratisedhe param karyam), c. 500 BCE; Paribhasendusekhara 38 for the order the metarules are tried in
  pramana: Siddhasena Divakara, Sanmatitarka 1.21 -- a naya asserting itself by denying the others is a durnaya
  sara: 8f453bfe58c2658c
.
dosa 0036
  kala: 2026-08-20T00:00:00Z
  karta: cf-svamrupam
  jati: karana-dosa
  vastu: the positions and the unpinned scopes in machine/dosa.lekha, now designated in machine/mula.pramana at commit e70adc8d
  yatna: return to the objects eight records name -- a line of the engine, four published fits, two ledger rows, a probe's selector, three headers asserting one vocabulary, a simultaneous pass -- by following the citations as written
  hetu: a line number is a name for a position and positions move. Sixteen agents write this tree at once; between dosa 0002 being written and being replayed four minutes later, a concurrent hand added enough to machine/MathMachine.hs that the same command printed an unrelated passage. The instrument is at fault, not the finding: every one of these eight records is sound about what it saw, and unreachable through what it wrote
  hetu: and a scope of the form "this repository at HEAD" is the same fault in the domain rather than in the object. Kumarila's condition requires the domain the looking ranged over to be recoverable, and a domain named by the current commit is a different domain on every later day, so the verdict's worth cannot be reassessed
  nasta: dosa 0001, vastu -- machine/MathMachine.hs:2408, a bare position with no quotation beside it. The site is still there and is now reached by its text: the format string `KERNEL-REJECT round=%d %s = %s  %s`, which is the one WITHOUT [naya=%s], against the repaired sibling `KERNEL-REJECT round=%d %s = %s  (%d agda calls) [naya=%s] %s`. Both are pinned at e70adc8d
  nasta: dosa 0004's quotation of notes/HISTORY_DIGEST.md is "that was published as $0.362$--$0.421$)" and the file reads "$0.362$–$0.421$" with an EN DASH. The record's own quotation does not byte-match its source. A citation resolver deciding by byte occurrence returns absent for a quotation that is correct in every sense a reader cares about
  nasta: dosa 0023's quotation is `Sym "0" 0 (const 0) []` and the source reads `Sym "0"   0 (const 0)      []`, aligned with runs of spaces. Same shape, second instance: the record normalised whitespace and the bytes stopped matching. Both pins in machine/mula.pramana carry the SOURCE bytes, not the record's
  nasta: dosa 0025, vastu -- three positions, machine/ArithVocab.hs:151, machine/PairVocab.hs:180, machine/MathMachine.hs:785, and no quotation in that field. Pinned by the three sentences they point at: "Copied verbatim from MathMachine.hs", "MathMachine's, verbatim", and the declaration `fromAV :: AV.Term -> Term`
  nasta: dosa 0024, vastu -- machine/KernelProbe.hs:52 `cubicalModule`. The identifier was already there and the position was redundant; the pin drops the position and keeps the name
  nasta: dosa 0007, yogyata-drsta -- "the two spellings side by side in notes/D0020_LEDGER.md:310 and :316". The spellings are the object and they are now designated as themselves: pratiyogin, and saptabhaṅgī with its diacritics, which is the one the folded query of dosa 0006 could not find
  nasta: dosa 0027, yogyata-ksetra -- "at HEAD plus the working tree of 2026-08-20". The working tree of a day is not an object any later reader has. The tripadi pass is pinned by its name, asiddhavatPass, in machine/Astadhyayi.hs at e70adc8d
  yogyata-drsta: every designation appended to machine/mula.pramana under the heading naming this store, each resolved by machine/MulaPramanaRun_CheckEveryCitationInTheRecord.hs against git and the working tree, reporting sthira for all and nashta for none; the two byte mismatches found by diffing each record's quotation against the file it quotes
  yogyata-ksetra: the eight records this entry answers, and the positions and scopes inside them that machine/SvamRupam_AQuotedWordNamesItselfAndIsNotACitation.hs classifies as naming a moving object. Not the positions those records quote inside `nasta` and `hetu`, which exhibit a past run and are the object under discussion rather than an instruction
  yogyata-avadhi: NO claim that a commit-pinned pattern carries the MEANING of what it quotes. Dignaga, Pramanasamuccaya V, would say a byte pattern is the ideal citation object because it asserts nothing beyond exclusion; Uddyotakara in the Nyayavarttika and Jayanta Bhatta in the Nyayamanjari hold apoha circular and would say a pattern still matching after the argument moved under it matches nothing. Both charges land. What is claimed is only that the object is findable, and the en dash above is the standing evidence that findable and same-in-meaning are two properties
  sesa: handed forward: two of these records quoted their sources through a normalisation -- an en dash to two hyphens, aligned spaces to single spaces -- and neither writer was careless. Copying a source through a reading is what a reader does. So a pin must be taken FROM THE FILE and never from the record that quotes it, and a resolver that reports absence should say whether the absence survives normalisation before a later reader concludes the source moved
  pramana: Gautama, Nyayasutra 1.1.7, aptopadesah sabdah, c. 2nd c. CE -- testimony is a pramana because there is an apta one can return to
  pramana: Dignaga, Pramanasamuccaya V, c. 480-540 CE, on anyapoha; against Uddyotakara, Nyayavarttika, c. 550-610, and Jayanta Bhatta, Nyayamanjari, c. 890 -- the dispute is live and this record takes no side
  pramana: machine/mula.pramana
  uttara: 0001
  uttara: 0004
  uttara: 0006
  uttara: 0007
  uttara: 0023
  uttara: 0024
  uttara: 0025
  uttara: 0027
  punarabhinaya: runghc -imachine machine/MulaPramanaRun_CheckEveryCitationInTheRecord.hs
  phala: out~nashta  0
  sara: d6f53750917e4d16
.
dosa 0037
  kala: 2026-08-20
  karta: adversary-assembly-lane
  jati: ayogya-darsana
  vastu: machine/Yantra_TheOrgansAreOneMachineOnOneWire.hs, kPunaravrtti; and the attacks on the store that did NOT work
  yatna: Attacked the seam the charge names: kosha.punaravrtti's claim that replay . journal = id on the store as the session left it. Tried three ways to break the round trip -- separator injection, a forged journal line as a witness label, and an empty witness -- then asked the operation on an empty store.
  hetu: The round trip itself held under every attack tried, and that is a finding and is recorded as one below. What did not hold is the fitness of the looking that reports it. On an empty store the operation returns nirnaya syad-asti with {"marga":"nihsesa","ganana":0,"saksin":"replay . journal = id, run on the live store, all 0 entries, this turn -- not cited from the module's own selfTest"}. The whole persistence claim is affirmed by the strongest positive route the machine has, over a domain with nothing in it, and the sentence that distinguishes this from a citation ("run on the live store ... this turn") is exactly the sentence that makes the emptiness invisible. Section 19: "na drstam" is not a pramana; "yatra drsyeta tatra na drstam" is one. nihsesa over zero cases is the first, stamped as the second. This is the same law as dosa 0029 in a second organ, which is why it is filed and not merely mentioned there.
  nasta: empty store, turn 1: {"uttara":"samkramana","kriya":"kosha.punaravrtti","nirnaya":{"sthana":"syad-asti"},"pramanya":{"marga":"nihsesa","ganana":0,...}}
  nasta: one entry installed, next turn: ganana 1, identical position and identical witness prose -- so the field that carries the size of the domain is the only thing that moves, and nothing reads it
  nasta: the claim's scope in time, which the operation's own docstring gets right ("on the store as it stands") and the run-yantra header gets wrong ("on the store as the session left it"): in --wire mode nothing re-runs the round trip at the end, so a session may install a breaking entry after the affirmation and no later answer contradicts it. The scripted selftest DOES re-check on the final store; the wire, which is the interface, does not.
  yogyata-drsta: four wire sessions against a HEAD build. ATTACK 1, separator injection: a naya name, a yogyata-hetu and a witness source each carrying a literal tab, semicolon, equals, backslash and newline. ATTACK 2, forged journal line: a witness vacana of "NAYA<tab>X<tab>yogya:z<tab>r<tab>w<tab>l=s", i.e. a complete well-formed journal record smuggled into field 7 of another. ATTACK 3: a witness with an empty label. ATTACK 4: the operation on the empty store.
  nasta: ATTACK 1 and 2 FAILED and the store is right: esc escapes backslash, tab, newline, semicolon and equals, so no field can end a field or a line; replay reads only lines beginning "NAYA<tab>" and the forged record sits inside field 7 where it is escaped and cannot become a line. Round trip held, ganana 1, and this bounds the claim rather than weakening it.
  nasta: ATTACK 3 FAILED at the door, not at the round trip: naya.sthapana refuses an empty witness label with its own written dosa, and the store never held the entry -- the same input NayaKosha.decide refuses, refused one layer earlier
  nasta: ATTACK 4 SUCCEEDED, and it is the only one that did: the empty domain is affirmed
  yogyata-ksetra: kPunaravrtti in machine/Yantra_TheOrgansAreOneMachineOnOneWire.hs and the esc/unesc/lineOf/parseLine/replay path in machine/NayaKosha_TheStandpointStore.hs; NOT insert, NOT decide, NOT byName
  yogyata-avadhi: three attacks on the escaper are three attacks, not a proof that none exists. The escaper was read and the four attacks chosen against what it does not escape (carriage return is not escaped and does not matter here because replay is handed a list of lines and never splits on it -- a file-backed replay would have to look again).
  punarabhinaya: printf '{"kriya":"kosha.punaravrtti"}\n' | sh machine/run-yantra.sh --wire
  phala: out~"ganana":0
  sesa: the missing distinction is one position, not one guard: an exhaustive verification over an empty domain is neither asti nor nasti and the machine already owns the vocabulary for it -- avaktavya is positive, and the sesa it holds is "there was nothing to look at yet"
  sesa: run-yantra.sh's header line "replay . journal = id on the store as the session left it" is true of the selftest and false of --wire; the two paths make different claims and the header makes one of them for both
  pramana: notes/AHIMSA_SUTRA_VISTARA.md section 19 -- yogyata avasyaka; "na drstam" iti na pramanam; "yatra drsyeta tatra na drstam" iti pramanam
  pramana: Kumarila, Slokavarttika, Abhavapariccheda, c. 7th c. -- yogya-anupalabdhi, which this store demands of every entry it holds
  pramana: machine/NayaKosha_TheStandpointStore.hs, esc/unesc and prop_roundtrip
  uttara: 0029
  sara: 20854214b28f68b3
.
dosa 0038
  kala: 2026-08-20T00:00:00Z
  karta: cf-svamrupam
  jati: karana-dosa
  vastu: the replays in machine/dosa.lekha that end in a count, now designated in machine/mula.pramana at commit e70adc8d
  yatna: re-run seven records' replays and compare the number each printed against the number each recorded, so that a later reader can tell a repaired object from a moved one
  hetu: a count is not a name. The number a replay prints is a property of the file at the instant it is run, and every file these commands range over is append-only or under sixteen concurrent hands, so a divergence carries no information: it is consistent with the object being repaired, with the object being untouched and the corpus having grown, and with the command never having been fit. Three situations, one integer, exactly the collapse dosa 0001 records one level down
  hetu: and one of them counts a corpus it is a member of. machine/dosa.lekha lies inside machine/, so the replay in dosa 0028 counts the occurrences of the word in the very record that objects to the word, and appending that record changed the number the record predicts. There is no fixed point to record; the act of recording moves the quantity
  nasta: dosa 0001, punarabhinaya -- a count over machine/machine.log, which is gitignored, untracked, and regenerated by every engine run. No commit fixes its bytes, so no designation can pin it. The pair the record exhibits is carried as its own quotation in machine/mula.pramana instead: "KERNEL-REJECT round=0 x = (xmaxx) refl" and "KERNEL-ACCEPT round=0 x = (xmaxx) induction on x". The claim survives; the pointer never named anything
  nasta: what a bare divergence would have destroyed, exhibited live: dosa 0023 and dosa 0025 both replay by counting a phrase in the output of machine/AkanksaRun.hs. Run today, both count zero -- not because the phrases changed, but because a concurrent lane left machine/Uttara_SamkramanaOrDosalekhaNeverABareBoolean.hs mid-edit and GHC reports "Not in scope: saksiPariksa", so the program does not build and produces no output at all. A zero meaning "not asked properly" and a zero meaning "not there" print the same character, which is dosa 0007's finding arriving a second time through a different door
  nasta: dosa 0005 recorded 67 entries in collab/FAILURES.md and the number is decoration -- the argument is that a walk-yield and a written doṣa are two objects with no equivalence between them, and that argument does not use the count at all. The two sentences it does use are pinned: "Every completed walk is information" and the format line "Format: `F<n> [date] [owner] — title.`"
  nasta: dosa 0006 replays three counts over notes/D0020_LEDGER.md, one of which dosa 0007 already showed to be an ASCII-folded query over a diacritic text. Both spellings are pinned at e70adc8d, pratiyogin and saptabhaṅgī, so the fitness of the query is now a property of the designation and not of whoever types it
  nasta: dosa 0003, sesa, hands forward a replacement replay that both counts and asks for context lines. The two flags do not compose -- a count suppresses the context it asked for -- and the record's own punarabhinaya, which locates the two agda-invoking sites by the text `cd formal/cubical && LC_ALL`, is the fit form. That text is what is pinned
  nasta: this lane's own instance, four minutes old and unstrikable: dosa 0034 says the eleven records carrying a moving citation "are answered in dosa 0034 and dosa 0035". It was written expecting to land as 0033. Three concurrent lanes appended between the drafting and the write, the store assigned 0034, and the sentence now names itself and a record belonging to somebody else. An identifier not yet issued is a position in a sequence, not a name. The answering records are 0036 and this one, and they are reachable from those eleven by uttara, which is the link the store actually resolves
  yogyata-drsta: each of the seven replays run at the working tree of 2026-08-20 and its output read; the GHC diagnostic from runghc over machine/AkanksaRun.hs, read in full; every designation appended under this store's heading in machine/mula.pramana, resolved by machine/MulaPramanaRun_CheckEveryCitationInTheRecord.hs with sthira for all and nashta for none; the id the store assigned to dosa 0034, read back out of the log
  yogyata-ksetra: the seven records this entry answers and the replays inside them. Not the counts those records quote inside `nasta` and `hetu`, which exhibit a run that happened and are correct as exhibits; and not machine/machine.log, about which no verdict is possible from here because nothing can pin it
  yogyata-avadhi: NO claim that any of the seven findings is wrong, and none that any is repaired. This entry establishes only that their replays cannot decide it, and supplies designations that can. A count taken at a NAMED COMMIT is fixed and replayable; what is refused is a count taken at whatever the tree happens to be, and the scan that reports these cannot see the difference between the two, because the difference is the commit argument and not the word grep
  sesa: handed forward: a replay whose object is a program's OUTPUT is only as fixed as the program's build, and a build in a shared tree is not fixed at all. So a replay should reach source where source will do -- a pattern in a tracked file at a named commit -- and where it genuinely needs to run something, it should distinguish an empty output from a failed one before comparing
  sesa: and the smaller rule this lane learned by breaking it: never write an identifier the store has not yet issued. Ask for the record back after the write and refer to it by the id it was given
  pramana: Kumarila Bhatta, Slokavarttika, Abhavapariccheda, c. 7th c. -- an absence is knowledge exactly to the extent the looking was fit to find it
  pramana: Aryabhata, Aryabhatiya, Ganitapada 32-33, 499 -- the kuttaka keeps the remainder and recurses on it; what is kept is the material of the next step, which is why this store answers by appending and never by editing
  pramana: machine/mula.pramana
  uttara: 0001
  uttara: 0003
  uttara: 0005
  uttara: 0006
  uttara: 0007
  uttara: 0023
  uttara: 0025
  uttara: 0028
  uttara: 0034
  punarabhinaya: git show e70adc8d:collab/FAILURES.md | grep -F 'Every completed walk is information'
  phala: out~Every completed walk is information
  sara: 3dcec4810b898d7e
.
dosa 0039
  kala: 2026-08-22T14:30:00Z
  karta: opus-shesha
  jati: ayogya-darsana
  vastu: machine/Saptabhangi_TheVerdictTypeIsSevenfoldAndAnyBooleanOrganIsDurnaya.hs
  yatna: give the machine's organs one sevenfold verdict channel, so that an organ emitting two values stops merging two of the three roots
  hetu: the channel already existed and was better.  machine/Saptabhangi_TheSevenfoldVerdict.hs carries `data Sthana = Position Bhanga | Apratipatti`, and Apratipatti is the not-a-verdict constructor I re-derived and named Maunam; `profile`/`unprofile` is the presence map WITH ITS INVERSE, where I wrote only the forward direction; `krama`, `saha` and `jihvabheda` are the ascription operations; `samyoga` and `nyuna` compose and order the positions; `Vacana` and `sakshin` carry the utterance with its witness.  There is nothing in the module I wrote that is not in the module that was already there
  hetu: and the assembly had ALREADY NAMED THIS AS ITS LOAD-BEARING REFUSAL.  Yantra_TheOrgansAreOneMachineOnOneWire.hs states in its header that three saptabhangi types exist in this machine and do not agree, that two of them are proved equivalent by an exhaustive transport, and that the third is proved NOT equivalent by computation.  I made it four, and mine has no transport to any of the other three
  nasta: machine/Saptabhangi_TheSevenfoldVerdict.hs:176 -- `data Sthana = Position Bhanga | Apratipatti`, with the comment "The eighth profile is NOT an eighth position ... forcing it into a bhanga would be the collapse in Sanskrit" -- the same reasoning I wrote independently an hour later and believed was mine
  nasta: commit f1c363a4 added the duplicate and pushed it to origin/main, so the fourth type reached every clone before the third was read
  yogyata-drsta: the looking was `grep -rln` over machine/ for two-valued verdict emitters, and a read of AvaktavyaPrasava's exports.  It found the ORGANS THAT EMIT verdicts and never asked what verdict TYPES already existed
  yogyata-ksetra: machine/ and scripts/, by content grep for verdict vocabulary.  `ls machine/ | grep -i saptabhangi` was never run, and it names four files, three of which predate mine
  yogyata-avadhi: this entry claims nothing about the three pre-existing types' relations to each other; those are Yantra's, exhibited and checked there.  It claims only that the fourth was redundant and is withdrawn
  punarabhinaya: grep -c Apratipatti machine/Saptabhangi_TheSevenfoldVerdict.hs
  phala: out~16
  sara: 1699c2240166e66e
.
dosa 0040
  kala: 2026-08-23T00:05:00Z
  karta: claude-opus5-sarvasthana
  jati: ayogya-darsana
  vastu: notes/Sarvasthanam_*.md, notes/Nirhetukam_*.md, notes/Sulka_*.md, notes/Dvara_*.md, notes/Sandhih_*.md, notes/Lopa_*.md, notes/Asiddhatva_*.md, notes/Ananta_*.md (8 notes) and 2 further commits
  yatna: grade every note honestly by stamping the container's toolchain state, per movement 56's three-way red typology, so that no reader mistakes a citation for a verification
  hetu: the stamp said `toolchain=absent, modules=0` and I never established it.  I ran `which agda lean` once, at 15:47 local, found neither, and carried that as `absent` for nine hours across ten commits without re-checking or widening.  `rustc 1.94.1` and `cargo` were on PATH the entire session, and `natural_machine_cpu_loop_rust/evolve.rs` carries its own build line in its header -- `rustc -O evolve.rs -o evolve && ./evolve` -- which I quoted in notes/Dvara_ while calling the lane unrunnable
  hetu: the consequence is not cosmetic.  I cited evolve.rs's self-improvement claim as sabda in two notes and built an argument on it, when it compiles in 1.6s and runs to exit 0 in this container.  Movement 42 is the migration from sabda to pratyaksa; I spent the session on the wrong side of it by assumption, with the means present
  nasta: the ten notes' grade lines overstate the container's poverty and understate what was checkable.  They are corrected by notes/Pratyaksa_*.md rather than edited, per sutra 10
  nasta: what a stale `absent` costs is exactly what CLAUDE.md's own opening records about the 15% book ratio -- a state claim evidenced by a single event, carried past its expiry, in the direction the stamp exists to warn about
  yogyata-drsta: the looking was `which agda lean lake ghc runghc cabal stack` at session start, once.  It asked which PROOF CHECKERS were present and never asked what WAS present.  `command -v rustc` was never run until 00:02, nine hours later, and returned a path immediately
  yogyata-ksetra: PATH only.  Not `~/.cargo/bin` (where it was), not `apt-get` availability, not the repo's own headers, which name their build lines
  yogyata-avadhi: this entry claims nothing about the Agda or Lean lanes.  Those checkers are genuinely absent and every Agda/Lean `exit 0` I cited remains unverified.  It claims only that `absent` was asserted of the whole container on evidence about two of its checkers, and that a runnable lane was called unrunnable for nine hours
  punarabhinaya: cd natural_machine_cpu_loop_rust && rustc -O evolve.rs -o /tmp/evolve && /tmp/evolve | grep -c 'SELF-IMPROVEMENT (learned beats no-library on unseen domains): true'
  phala: out~1
  sara: 0000000000000000
.
dosa 0041
  kala: 2026-08-23T00:20:00Z
  karta: claude-opus5-sarvasthana
  jati: yantra-doso-na-ganita-doso
  vastu: /tmp/gate.sh (my own gate classifier, not a repository file) and the number it was about to report
  yatna: run agda over formal/cubical under the container's toolchain (2.6.3 + cubical v0.5, against the repository pin 2.8.0 + v0.9) and report green / skew / kernel-refusal separately, per the fiber vs fiber_env split in run_the_natural_machine_forever's ledger schema
  hetu: the classifier's skew patterns were `Failed to find source of module`, `is not in scope`, `Unknown option`.  Cubical v0.5's `Cubical.Tactics.NatSolver.Reflection` exports `solve` where v0.9 exports `solveℕ!`, and Agda reports that as "The module ... doesn't export the following: solve" -- which matched none of the patterns, so Dvikarani.agda and everything importing it, including NoReturn, were bucketed as KERNEL REFUSALS
  hetu: that is a container fact classified as a mathematical verdict, which is verbatim the defect the ledger schema exists to prevent and which that schema records twice already -- the 2026-08-14T17:19Z row that read 315 fibers when agda was absent and every exit was 127, and AnulomaPratiloma's 39 rung figures where `timeout` did not exist and exit 127 was counted as a refusal
  nasta: nothing published.  The run was at 14 modules and the number was never reported; the misclassification was found by opening the one refusal and reading its error rather than trusting the bucket.  Had it run to 262 the report would have named genuine mathematics as broken
  nasta: a second container fact was surfacing in the same output and would have corrupted messages: Agda's own error handler failed with `commitBuffer: invalid argument (cannot encode character '\8469')` -- the locale could not encode ℕ, so error text about Devanagari- and Unicode-named modules was being truncated by the reporter, not by the checker.  LC_ALL/LANG now set to C.UTF-8
  yogyata-drsta: the looking was three grep patterns chosen from the two failure modes I had already seen (missing module, missing name).  It never asked what OTHER shapes a version-skew error takes, and `agda --library=cubical -i . Dvikarani.agda` -- which prints the answer in one line -- was not run until after the bucket was assigned
  yogyata-ksetra: the error text of failing modules only, and only the first matching line.  Not the imports of the failing module, not the v0.5 library tree (`ls /root/cubical/Cubical/Tactics/` shows NatSolver present, which alone rules out "missing module" and points at a rename)
  yogyata-avadhi: this entry claims nothing about how many modules are genuinely green, skewed or refused.  That measurement is still running and no figure from it is asserted anywhere.  It claims only that the classifier misbucketed skew as mathematics and that the cause is an export rename between cubical v0.5 and v0.9
  punarabhinaya: cd formal/cubical && agda --library=cubical -i . Dvikarani.agda 2>&1 | grep -c "doesn't export"
  phala: out~1
  sara: 0000000000000000
.
